WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:58.560
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.500
BACOW: Good evening.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.410
My name is Larry Bacow, and as
Chancellor of the Institute,

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.090
it's my pleasure
to welcome you all

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:07.900
to the inaugural lecture in
the Ford Nobel Laureate Lecture

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.340
Series.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.010
This series will bring
Nobel laureates to MIT

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:13.900
from throughout the
world to discuss

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.270
important work in their fields.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.030
All of the fields in which
Nobel Prizes are awarded

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.130
will be represented
in this series.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:22.990
Ford Nobel lectures
will be scheduled

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.300
throughout the fall
and the spring terms

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.580
over the next five years.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:30.430
We're very grateful
to Ford Motor Company

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:32.410
for sponsoring this series.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.330
It's part of an
extensive partnership

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:37.420
between Ford and
MIT which focuses

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.670
on research and education.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.460
MIT faculty and students
are working collaboratively

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.790
with Ford in a variety of
fields that encompass virtually

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:48.140
all five of MIT schools.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:52.390
We're collaborating on projects
as diverse as the environment,

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:54.970
e-commerce, combustion
engineering,

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:58.170
virtual engineering,
and virtual education.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.510
This partnership is helping to
define new models of university

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:05.240
industry collaboration and
we're very proud of it.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.510
It had been my plan at
this point in the evening

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.940
to introduce Dr. Martin
Zimmerman, the vice

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.120
president for government
affairs at Ford who was going

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.220
to introduce our speakers.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.440
Unfortunately, Marty's
plane has been delayed.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.380
And he'll be joining us
later on in the program.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.850
So it becomes my pleasure to
introduce our three speakers.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.380
I've invited Marty
when he gets here

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.380
to come up and actually
close the program

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.970
and thank our panelists.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:32.350
Since I won't have
an opportunity

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.130
to properly introduce
him at the time,

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.400
just let me tell you a
little bit about Marty.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.740
He's an alumnus of our
economics department

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.830
where he received his
PhD, former faculty member

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.209
and our Sloan School,
after which he went on

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:46.750
to become a faculty member at
the Graduate School of Business

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:48.640
at the University of Michigan.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.340
He's been a wonderful
friend of the Institute

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.400
and has played a major role in
helping to create the Ford MIT

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.330
partnership.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.830
And we're very
grateful for his help

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.130
and in sponsoring
this event today.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:04.330
Our three speakers tonight
really need no introduction.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:09.010
Now Marty told me when I
spoke to him earlier today

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.440
that he once said that when
he was about to introduce

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.420
one of his bosses.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.660
And the response was, well,
you know, that's true,

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.460
but I prefer if you go
ahead and did it anyway.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:25.150
So let me just say a few words
about Paul Samuelson, Franco

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.510
Modigliani, and Bob Solow.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:31.000
If I really tried to
describe all of their honors

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.430
and awards and honorary
degrees, we really

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:35.950
wouldn't have time for
the interesting work

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.320
of this evening, which
is their remarks.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.140
So I will be brief.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.750
Professor Samuelson received
the Nobel Memorial Prize

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.699
in economic science in 1970
for the scientific work

00:03:47.699 --> 00:03:49.240
through which he
has developed static

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.330
and dynamic economic theory
and actively contributed

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.090
to raising the level of
analyses in economic science.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:58.290
That's a direct quotation
from the Nobel citation.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.210
As Assar Lindbeck said in
the presentation speech,

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.310
Professor Samuelson
has, in fact, simply

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:06.930
rewritten considerable
parts of economic theory.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.400
He has also shown
the fundamental unity

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.150
of both the problems and
analytical techniques

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:14.320
in economics.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.450
Any introduction of
Professor Samuelson

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.970
would not be complete
without noting

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:22.360
that he is the author
of what I believe

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:28.450
is the most popular textbook
in the history of not just

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:32.260
economics but all of
American higher education.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.260
Professor Modigliani received
the Nobel Memorial Prize

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:38.410
in economic science
in 1985 for quote,

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.450
"his pioneering
analyses of savings

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.400
and of financial markets."

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.470
The achievements for
which he was recognized

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:48.100
included the life-cycle
hypothesis of household savings

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.090
and for the formulation of
the Modigliani-Miller theorems

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:56.140
of the valuation of firms
and of capital costs.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:00.520
Franco has been a consultant
and involved with governments

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.810
throughout the world.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:06.160
And we have all been very
grateful for his contributions

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.550
to our economics department as
with Paul for many, many years.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.640
Professor Solow received
the Nobel Memorial Prize

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:16.750
in economic science in
1987 for his contributions

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.910
to the theory of
economic growth.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.700
The Nobel Committee cited
the enormous influence

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:23.800
of the Solow growth
model and his work

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.320
on empirical growth analysis.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.970
And, indeed, as we
come to understand

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:30.910
the role of technological change
and productivity and the role

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.520
that it is playing in
our economy today, again,

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:37.030
we turn to the
work of Bob Solow.

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:40.720
Now, the Nobel Prize
recognizes the contributions

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:43.580
of these three men to the
field of economic science.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.510
It really is awarded
for their research.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:48.850
But in point of fact,
beyond their research,

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:51.880
each of these men have
made absolutely enormous

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:56.640
contributions to the education
of generations of economists,

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.074
not just at MIT, but
throughout the world.

00:05:59.074 --> 00:06:00.740
Indeed, I don't think
I'm overstating it

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:03.710
when I say that they
are at least as loved

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:06.950
and as is well-regarded
for the wonderful work

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:10.700
they have done as members
of our economics faculty

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.850
as they are for
their contributions

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:15.440
to economic science.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.780
So tonight, they are going to
take the long view, both back

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:25.500
and forward, taking a look at
our economy the last 50 years--

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.950
and what I'm sure
everybody's anxious to hear--

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:31.400
their prognostication of the
economy for the next 50 years.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:34.910
Please join me
again in welcoming

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:38.450
three giants of our field, Paul
Samuelson, Franco Modigliani,

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:39.639
and Bob Solow.

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:43.551
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:01.610
SAMUELSON: The last time I saw
this hall full to overflowing

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:08.570
was when I had the duty
to debate Milton Friedman.

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:15.710
It was a question of one
lion and one Christian.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:20.480
And I will not tell you
which part was mine.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.090
Tonight, I expect, will
be a piece of cake.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:28.670
All I have to do is
to discuss with you

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:34.110
the major economic problems
in the upcoming decade.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.720
When I first wrote my
best selling economics

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.890
textbook at World War
II's end, I correctly

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:44.940
identified what would be
the major economic problem

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:46.840
of the upcoming
quarter of a century.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:53.910
Simply put, it was avoiding
the depression, unemployment

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:57.620
that blighted the 1930s.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:58.880
That was then.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.940
Now is 2000.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:06.970
My choice now, looking ahead,
not to a quarter of a century

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:12.780
but just for a decade, is
what is the major problem?

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:16.890
And I believe it to
be how to work out

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:20.220
the Aristotelian
golden mean that

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:24.500
weighs the delicate compromise
between what we render

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:27.720
to the market price
mechanism and what

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:32.159
we render to democratic
government interferences

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:35.010
by the democratic welfare state.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:38.730
And my remarks build
up to that problem.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:46.050
Back at the end of World War
II, all the standard textbooks

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:52.620
were still stuck in the 1920s
orthodoxy of pure capitalism.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:53.700
I knew that system.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:56.500
I grew up under that system.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:00.430
I sold Norman Rockwell
Saturday Evening Posts.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:01.770
There were no unions.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:07.930
Up to the time of my infancy,
childhood, and adolescence,

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:10.900
that was the system.

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:15.750
But after the Herbert
Hoover slump--

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:23.730
that is the only partisan remark
I will make in the evening--

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:27.210
MIT students and college
beginners everywhere

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:31.080
needed to learn the
Keynesian macroeconomics that

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:35.340
ushered in a generation of
unprecedented global growth

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:38.510
in the third quarter
of the 20th century.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:42.300
For better or worse,
Franklin Roosevelt's New Deal

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.910
moved us into a mixed economy--

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.720
not pure capitalism, certainly
not bureaucratic command

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:52.530
communism of the
Stalin and Mao type.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:55.350
Here at home and
similarly abroad almost

00:09:55.350 --> 00:10:00.450
everywhere, the quasi
welfare state evolved.

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:04.020
Now what does that
mean concretely?

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:07.620
It means most of the
business of the society,

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:13.440
the economic business is left to
the allocation of market price

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.200
mechanism--

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:20.700
profit, loss, selfishness,
checks and balances.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:24.740
First this was, so to speak,
a Monday to Friday affair.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:28.340
But then on Sunday and,
increasingly, even on Saturday,

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:33.740
democratic governmental
legislatures and agencies

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:37.790
did lean against the wind of the
glaring inequalities generated

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:43.420
by the competitive price
private property mechanism.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:45.510
Well, how did they
lean against the wind?

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.780
It wasn't old fashioned
socialism, government ownership

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:50.670
of the means of production.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:55.350
It was done, as far as the
redistribution of income,

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:59.370
to attenuate the worst
inequalities, which will always

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:06.170
be generated by a laissez-faire
private profit system.

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:09.410
That has to be done
by the fiscal system.

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:12.860
Progressive taxation takes
more from the rich than

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:14.150
from the poor.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:17.060
Redistributive
transfer expenditures,

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:19.740
unemployment insurance,
social security, poverty,

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:23.720
and welfare programs,
bank deposit insurance,

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:26.570
give to the poor and
lower income classes

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:30.140
more than it takes
from them by taxes.

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:34.940
Well, I won't use time
to describe that process

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:37.460
because you've
all lived in that.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:38.660
You were born into it.

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:41.490
You've grown up into it.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:45.830
However, about in the third
quarter of the century,

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:48.410
towards the last
part of the century,

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:53.090
nature cannot stand relative
prosperity and stability

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:55.190
and complacency.

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:58.460
By the quarter of the
century just ended,

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:02.480
in much of the world there has
grown up a conservative trend

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:05.030
away from altruism.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:08.150
That is a fact about
modern democracy,

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:11.860
a different fact from the
laissez-faire society--

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:15.260
I'm sorry from the
1929 drift away

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:18.230
from the laissez-faire society.

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:21.270
And you've seen it
all over the globe.

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:25.490
You've seen it in presidencies
of Eisenhower, Nixon, Reagan,

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:27.230
elder George Bush.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:31.070
Even in Scandinavia
and continental Europe

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:34.940
social democracies
retreated under attack.

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:41.380
Now, let me give you my take
on the two reasons for this.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:44.530
It's axiomatic that
always the affluent

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:47.320
will hope to use their
money power to resist

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:49.660
populist demands to
have the government help

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:53.890
the masses at their expense.

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:56.830
I'm speaking of my
suburban neighbors,

00:12:56.830 --> 00:13:01.180
people you all know,
mostly, actually,

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:04.910
your own parents, yeah.

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:09.520
However, before Keynes
showed us how to mostly tame

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:14.140
depressions the lower
3/4 of the income classes

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:17.530
of the electorate suffered
enough to goad them

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:22.090
into opposing the
disproportionate powers

00:13:22.090 --> 00:13:27.160
in democratic real politic
of private business

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:29.380
and of affluence.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:33.010
Our system is one
nose, one vote.

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:37.390
But our system of
how the votes count

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:40.660
is that if you have lots
of dollars in your wallet,

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:44.710
your effective voting
power will be greater.

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:49.770
So in the age after Keynes,
discontent atrophied

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:53.400
and lobbyists learned
how to operate.

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:58.530
Also many, from even the lowest
part of the middle classes,

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:00.960
came to overvalue their
own lottery ticket

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:06.960
probability of being one of
the rich favored by politics.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.390
If you're taking
notes, I hope you'll

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:15.180
rewrite those tongue-tied
last couple of sentences.

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:17.040
But there was a
second possible reason

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:22.530
for the retreat from
voter-imposed altruism.

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:25.350
We do-gooders are
like Oliver Twist.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.570
We always ask for more.

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.984
We are like the boy who
said he could spell banana

00:14:29.984 --> 00:14:31.650
except that he didn't
know when to stop.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:39.450
Going all the way, as
Lenin, Stalin, and Mao did,

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:42.810
was motivated, said
it was motivated

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:46.470
by good, if
unrealistic intentions.

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:50.460
But I'm not speaking of
communism and the recent ending

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:52.020
of the Cold War.

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:55.590
I'm speaking of societies
like those in Scandinavia,

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:58.530
in Israel, all over the world.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:02.820
Once modern democracies
began to spend and organize

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:08.130
more than 50% of society's
potential GDP two things

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:10.360
seem to begin to happen.

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:15.520
Now, I'm giving you my
slant, my reading of events.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:20.200
High tax rates do begin
to put considerable sand

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.530
in the economic cogwheels.

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:25.450
The mathematical
economist discovers

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:28.330
that these deadweight losses
against the efficiency

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:31.300
and growth progress will
increase with the square

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:33.800
of the altruistic scale.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:37.430
I'm talking MIT talk.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:40.070
I do not deny that
any noble cause should

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:44.090
be pushed some distance into
the law of diminishing returns.

00:15:44.090 --> 00:15:47.780
But remember, we have
only one human nature.

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:50.840
It's that which evolved
from the Darwinian struggle

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:52.490
for existence.

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:56.150
And it seems to have
left us, all of us,

00:15:56.150 --> 00:16:00.980
with only a limited endowment
of this precious altruism.

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:03.410
So altruism, I
believe, does need

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:06.680
to be rationed
carefully precisely

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:10.540
because of its scarcity.

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:15.070
Well, that's not all
about big government.

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:20.370
I started out as a--

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:21.720
how shall I describe myself.

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:26.510
I was trained by Jesuits at
the University of Chicago.

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:31.980
The cathedral of choice at
my University of Chicago

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:35.190
was the cathedral of
free private enterprise,

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:39.810
not of the medieval
Catholic church.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:43.920
But once I threw off
that brainwashing--

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:46.430
because I was like a sponge,
I was a very good student,

00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:47.610
so I was a sponge--

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:57.000
I reached a balanced
value judgment position.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.760
Now, has it changed?

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:02.490
My arteries have hardened.

00:17:02.490 --> 00:17:05.940
Have I followed the
usual course of becoming

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:09.960
more conservative as I age?

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:13.410
Now, I'm the greatest
expert on me.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.900
So I would just tell
you that I don't

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:21.089
detect in myself very much
of a great change in value

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:23.210
judgments.

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:28.160
However I haven't been
asleep all these years.

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:35.140
And it was a great event for me
when Joseph McCarthy undertook

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:38.440
a witch hunt in
the United States

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:42.850
which reached deep into
every governmental circle,

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:47.380
deep into every academic group.

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:53.170
And I suddenly realized
what one big employer means.

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:57.750
When innocent friends of mine
were blackballed and lost

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:03.340
their job, if we'd gone
all the way, if we really

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:06.790
were a one employing
company, there

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:09.490
would be no place to hide.

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:11.080
They went to work
for hardware stores.

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:15.610
One became a treasure
of Mars candy company.

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:19.930
There is a certain
protection for all

00:18:19.930 --> 00:18:26.140
of the do-gooder liberal
things that I treasure

00:18:26.140 --> 00:18:31.900
in not having one big company.

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:36.550
Moreover-- and this is an
economic finding that I cannot

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:38.710
document precisely--

00:18:38.710 --> 00:18:41.710
but something seems
to happen when even

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:45.460
in a very civilized part of
the world like Scandinavia

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:49.930
that part of the GDP that
goes through government hands

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:57.880
becomes 50%, 60%, when tax rates
become 92% out of every dollar,

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:01.150
you don't teach much
night school or summer

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:04.540
school under those conditions.

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:08.560
It isn't just that sand in the
cogs that I spoke of earlier.

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:11.380
But there is
something that becomes

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:16.300
unresponsive to human
needs in the decision

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:20.500
making of the
lobbyist, politician,

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:25.270
world that leads me
to the conclusion

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:27.970
that whereas I used
to in my MIT class

00:19:27.970 --> 00:19:33.400
speak of the mixed economy,
of the welfare state,

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:39.190
I now speak of the limited mixed
economy, the limited welfare

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:40.370
state.

00:19:40.370 --> 00:19:45.430
And so that brings me up to the
topic that I started out with.

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:53.770
What is the best compromise,
what are the best limits so

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:58.390
that we get the advantages
of the regulatory powers

00:19:58.390 --> 00:20:02.830
of democratic government,
of the stabilization powers

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:05.770
of a Federal Reserve
central bank,

00:20:05.770 --> 00:20:10.870
of a fiscal policy, which leans
against the wind when there are

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:15.680
no jobs it's the right
thing to lower taxes,

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:18.790
it's the right thing
to run deficits,

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:23.290
but it is not the right thing
to do those same things when

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:30.940
we are in a overheated, very
high employment economy?

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:34.210
Now, there are no simple rules.

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:37.750
Young assistant professors
will spend their lifetime

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:42.309
in working out documented
progress on that problem.

00:20:42.309 --> 00:20:43.975
And that, I think,
is the major problem.

00:20:43.975 --> 00:20:45.490
And maybe some of
it will come out

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:47.440
in the discussion that follows.

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:48.228
Thank you.

00:20:48.228 --> 00:21:00.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:03.640
SOLOW: When we drew
lots not so long ago,

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:08.750
the decision was that Paul
would speak first on this panel

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:12.910
and I second and
Franco Modigliani last.

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:14.980
So it's my turn now.

00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:18.910
And I'm going to talk about
slightly different things

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:24.290
from what you've just
heard from Paul Samuelson.

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:26.260
The first thing you
should keep in mind--

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:29.650
remember what the title
for this symposium

00:21:29.650 --> 00:21:34.120
was, the last 50 years
and the next 50 years--

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:41.410
you should keep in mind that
the years from 1950 to 2000

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:46.480
were, in most economic ways,
in this country extraordinarily

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:48.640
successful.

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:52.330
By the way, the period
from 1950 to 2000

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:54.880
fits my lifetime very well.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:59.860
1950 was the year in which
I finished my PhD thesis

00:21:59.860 --> 00:22:02.890
and began teaching at MIT.

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:07.660
So the next 50 years
will cover roughly

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:11.980
the corresponding period
in many of your lives.

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:15.090
The next 50 years are
the years between--

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:18.820
the graduate students
in the room--

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:26.320
between now and when you are
old and doddering like us.

00:22:30.490 --> 00:22:33.970
The question that was
raised, that we want to raise

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:36.730
and I want to raise
and discuss here

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:40.210
is you should be thinking
about is whether the next,

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:42.640
whether you will
have it so good,

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:49.090
whether the next 50 years can
be as successful as the last.

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:54.430
I don't want to bore you with
measurements of how much better

00:22:54.430 --> 00:22:59.830
or how well the country
did in the last 50 years.

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:07.030
But one simple statistic is
that real consumption spending

00:23:07.030 --> 00:23:10.030
per person in the
population, what

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:14.350
the average expenditure on
consumer goods and services

00:23:14.350 --> 00:23:19.600
corrected for inflation was,
in 2000, 3 and 1/2 times what

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:23.770
it was in 1950.

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:25.900
Measured that way,
the standard of living

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:29.770
multiplied itself by 3 and 1/2.

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:31.990
By the way, I was
a little shocked

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:39.010
to discover that in 1950, almost
15% of the American workforce

00:23:39.010 --> 00:23:41.470
was engaged in agriculture.

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:47.680
That number is now
between 2% and 3%.

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:52.480
But multiplying what command
over consumption goods

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:57.510
the average person has
by a factor of 3 and 1/2

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:01.840
is a pretty dramatic
transformation.

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:09.610
There were, however, failures
in that 50 year period.

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:12.670
First of all, the improvement in
the average standard of living

00:24:12.670 --> 00:24:15.730
was not smooth or uniform.

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:23.950
There were recessions in 1954,
in 1958, in 1960, in 1970,

00:24:23.950 --> 00:24:32.380
in 1975, in 1980, in
1982, and in 1991.

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:36.310
And you know the standard
newspaper puzzle,

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:41.020
what is the next term
in that progression?

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:44.950
You're not going to
learn that tonight.

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:50.590
There were also episodes of
inflation during that period.

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:55.630
Output per hour work,
productivity of labor in the US

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:59.860
grew rapidly early in the
period and then slowed down

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:06.330
and then may have sped up
again in the last few years.

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:11.750
A more worrying aspect of
that last 50 year period

00:25:11.750 --> 00:25:14.530
was that the amount
of inequality

00:25:14.530 --> 00:25:20.620
in the distribution of economic
goods was getting worse.

00:25:20.620 --> 00:25:25.870
Inequality diminished early
in that 50 year period.

00:25:25.870 --> 00:25:31.180
And then from about the
mid 1970s, turned around

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:36.550
and we had a long spell
of widening inequality,

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:41.350
a bigger spread between the
top and the bottom earners

00:25:41.350 --> 00:25:44.020
in the population.

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:49.990
It looks as if that worsening
inequality may have stopped

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:51.520
in the past two or three years.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:53.890
It's really too soon to know.

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:55.780
The data are fragmentary.

00:25:55.780 --> 00:25:59.140
And in any case, there has even,
in the past two or three years,

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:05.420
not been any reversal
of widening inequality.

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:09.690
It may be that
between 1950 and 2000,

00:26:09.690 --> 00:26:16.040
the incomes, the
real incomes of those

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:19.490
in the bottom third of
the income distribution

00:26:19.490 --> 00:26:21.760
may actually have decreased.

00:26:21.760 --> 00:26:23.510
By the way, those are
not the same people.

00:26:23.510 --> 00:26:25.718
The people who were in the
bottom third of the income

00:26:25.718 --> 00:26:28.970
distribution in 1950
are not the same people

00:26:28.970 --> 00:26:31.610
who were in the
bottom third in 2000.

00:26:31.610 --> 00:26:34.430
But if you think of that
as a role in society,

00:26:34.430 --> 00:26:41.270
being one of the 33% poorest
people in the society,

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:44.810
then the real income
associated with that role

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:48.920
has probably actually
diminished in the past,

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:51.860
in the past 50 years.

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:56.180
We know, for instance, that
the earnings premium associated

00:26:56.180 --> 00:26:59.780
with higher education has
increased and is still

00:26:59.780 --> 00:27:00.470
increasing.

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:07.450
That's very good
for you and for us.

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:10.850
As a matter of fact, the
US has been very successful

00:27:10.850 --> 00:27:12.830
at higher education.

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:19.790
People come from elsewhere to
go to universities in the US.

00:27:19.790 --> 00:27:24.950
We have not done very
well at educating

00:27:24.950 --> 00:27:29.510
the less talented part of
the population and the poor.

00:27:29.510 --> 00:27:31.910
People don't come
from far away to go

00:27:31.910 --> 00:27:41.870
to urban elementary schools
or high schools in the US.

00:27:41.870 --> 00:27:44.750
One of the bad things
about the past 50 years

00:27:44.750 --> 00:27:53.740
is that we risk perpetuating a
sort of permanent underclass.

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:58.860
But if the period from 1950
to 2000 was a success--

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:05.480
and I think despite the
problems that I mentioned,

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:13.520
that success was based mainly
on technological innovation,

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:18.050
improvements in economic
policy, and good luck,

00:28:18.050 --> 00:28:24.880
in what proportions
it's very hard to say.

00:28:24.880 --> 00:28:29.780
And this is, in a way, what
Paul was telling you about.

00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:35.230
Some of those, many of those
improvements in economic policy

00:28:35.230 --> 00:28:39.700
are probably traceable to
better economic analysis,

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:45.010
to a better understanding of
the way a mixed system like ours

00:28:45.010 --> 00:28:47.300
works.

00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:48.920
How about the next 50 years?

00:28:48.920 --> 00:28:51.390
How about the years
when you will be as old

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:55.720
as the three of us are now?

00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:59.530
No one can predict what
economic life in the US

00:28:59.530 --> 00:29:03.370
will be like in 2050.

00:29:03.370 --> 00:29:06.100
That's not because
we're bad at predicting.

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:07.780
It hasn't been determined yet.

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:11.650
God hasn't decided how good
things will be in 2050.

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:16.930
So how could we possibly know?

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:23.770
But there are some
fairly clear tendencies

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:25.040
that we can talk about.

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:29.980
For example, it's not just us,
but the population of the US

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:30.670
is aging.

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:41.050
And that's happening elsewhere
in the modern industrial world

00:29:41.050 --> 00:29:43.160
as well.

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:45.430
There will be, 50
years from now,

00:29:45.430 --> 00:29:48.490
relatively more
old people like us

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:53.020
and relatively fewer
young people like you.

00:29:53.020 --> 00:29:56.660
That comes about for
several reasons--

00:29:56.660 --> 00:30:01.660
first of all, because of
increasing longevity, also

00:30:01.660 --> 00:30:05.110
because of smaller
family size, there

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:08.320
are fewer children
born on the average

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:15.340
to each woman in
modern societies.

00:30:15.340 --> 00:30:17.650
And the aging of
the population also

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:20.440
has to do with a single
historical event, the baby

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:26.890
boom, the fact that about
1950, in fact, family sizes

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:31.870
and the size of a
completed family in the US

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:35.920
and elsewhere and the
world began to rise

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:40.170
and created a bulge
in the population.

00:30:40.170 --> 00:30:44.320
That bulge, like a pig
swallowed by a snake,

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:54.760
is sort of creeping through
the body politic and--

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:57.630
SAMUELSON: Stop there.

00:30:57.630 --> 00:30:59.820
SOLOW: Yes, I do not
intend to describe

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:01.110
the end of that process.

00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:13.155
But before, before 2050, the
baby boom will have retired.

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:19.700
And this very important
fact, that the population

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:23.660
is getting relatively
older, is unlikely to be

00:31:23.660 --> 00:31:25.730
reversed any time soon.

00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:30.110
Immigration is an offset to
the aging of the population.

00:31:30.110 --> 00:31:33.410
Immigrants tend to be
younger and more fertile,

00:31:33.410 --> 00:31:39.950
have more children than
resident Americans.

00:31:39.950 --> 00:31:42.470
But immigration is
unlikely to occur

00:31:42.470 --> 00:31:48.060
in anything like large enough
numbers to reverse the process.

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:52.590
Why is the aging of the
population important?

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:55.970
It's important because
the working age population

00:31:55.970 --> 00:32:01.430
shrinks as a proportion
of the total population.

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:04.280
If we want to look at the
standard of living at income

00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:07.520
per person in the population,
we can describe that

00:32:07.520 --> 00:32:09.170
as the product of two numbers--

00:32:09.170 --> 00:32:12.020
income per worker
and the number,

00:32:12.020 --> 00:32:15.240
the ratio of workers
to population.

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:17.720
And if the second
of those factors

00:32:17.720 --> 00:32:24.830
gets smaller, as it undoubtedly
will, the standard of living

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:29.600
will tend to suffer unless
something else happens.

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:32.510
The average standard of
living will fall unless--

00:32:32.510 --> 00:32:38.510
compared with what would have
been without this aging process

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:44.420
unless something
happens to change it.

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:46.700
We will simply have
fewer producers

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:49.190
relative to the
number of consumers.

00:32:49.190 --> 00:32:51.770
There will be non-economic
consequences of the aging

00:32:51.770 --> 00:32:53.990
of the population as well.

00:32:53.990 --> 00:32:57.530
Nursing homes are going to
be more popular than nursery

00:32:57.530 --> 00:32:59.750
schools relatively speaking.

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:02.330
And voters, voting will change.

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:06.980
Old voters are different
from young voters.

00:33:06.980 --> 00:33:09.610
By the way, you
want to keep in mind

00:33:09.610 --> 00:33:13.040
that this fact that I've
been describing to you

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:17.510
is, to a first approximation,
only to a first approximation,

00:33:17.510 --> 00:33:20.270
independent of social
security arrangements.

00:33:20.270 --> 00:33:23.270
Franco, unless I miss my guess,
will want to talk to you a bit

00:33:23.270 --> 00:33:27.350
about Social Security.

00:33:27.350 --> 00:33:30.350
But the main function of
the social security system

00:33:30.350 --> 00:33:34.250
is to determine how
much of the national pie

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:36.560
goes to the old
folks and how they

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:41.570
finance their access to their
share of the national pie.

00:33:41.570 --> 00:33:45.220
But what there is to
divide in any decade,

00:33:45.220 --> 00:33:49.290
in the decade from 2040
to 2050, for instance,

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:51.990
is what is produced
in that decade.

00:33:51.990 --> 00:33:56.120
The social security rolls
don't directly effect that.

00:33:56.120 --> 00:33:58.200
But they do indirectly.

00:33:58.200 --> 00:34:00.950
They do because the
social security rules

00:34:00.950 --> 00:34:02.840
influence retirement decisions.

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:06.920
They may provide incentives
to retire earlier or later.

00:34:06.920 --> 00:34:09.770
Older retirement would
relieve the aging problem.

00:34:12.389 --> 00:34:14.750
In fact, in recent years,
the age of retirement

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:17.449
has been decreasing,
not only in the US,

00:34:17.449 --> 00:34:21.110
but elsewhere in the
industrial world as well.

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:28.880
And that reinforces the
effect of an aging population.

00:34:28.880 --> 00:34:35.060
The real offset to having a
smaller working population

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:37.340
is higher productivity.

00:34:37.340 --> 00:34:40.070
We know that output
per hour work

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:46.719
will be higher in
2050 than it is now.

00:34:46.719 --> 00:34:49.929
An important question is
whether productivity growth

00:34:49.929 --> 00:34:54.350
can be accelerated as
a matter of policy,

00:34:54.350 --> 00:34:57.190
sort of as a conscious
decision of the society.

00:34:57.190 --> 00:34:58.990
The answer to that
is probably yes,

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:04.670
although it is really impossible
to be quantitative about it.

00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:06.370
And that brings me
to a question that I

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:08.930
think you would
like to talk about

00:35:08.930 --> 00:35:13.360
and I don't like to
talk about, the question

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:16.960
that you read in the newspapers
or on the web or wherever.

00:35:16.960 --> 00:35:19.930
Are we on the verge
of or are we already

00:35:19.930 --> 00:35:24.100
into some sort of new
industrial revolution,

00:35:24.100 --> 00:35:29.320
a new economy that will make the
kinds of mundane considerations

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:31.910
that I've been talking
about obsolete?

00:35:35.270 --> 00:35:38.200
A simpler question is
whether the acceleration

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:43.520
of productivity that has
happened between 1995

00:35:43.520 --> 00:35:47.800
and this year, and 2000,
whether that acceleration

00:35:47.800 --> 00:35:52.240
is sustainable for
the next 50 years?

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:56.380
By the way, even the rate
of productivity growth

00:35:56.380 --> 00:36:01.270
between 1995 and 2000
is not noticeably faster

00:36:01.270 --> 00:36:07.270
than it was between
1950 and 1972.

00:36:07.270 --> 00:36:10.960
In round numbers, output
per hour worked in the US

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:14.110
rose at a rate
between 1% and 1.5%

00:36:14.110 --> 00:36:19.340
a year in the years
between 1972 and 1995.

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:23.950
And in the five years or four
years between 1995 and 1999,

00:36:23.950 --> 00:36:26.560
it rose between
2% and 3% a year,

00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:31.270
and even faster so far in 2000.

00:36:31.270 --> 00:36:32.380
That's a big difference.

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:34.480
It seems like a
small difference.

00:36:34.480 --> 00:36:38.890
Does productivity go up at 1% a
year or 2% a year or 2% a year

00:36:38.890 --> 00:36:40.330
or 3% a year?

00:36:40.330 --> 00:36:44.320
But over a 50 year period,
1% productivity growth

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:51.070
increases productivity by
65%, roughly 2% by 170%,

00:36:51.070 --> 00:36:55.780
and 3% by 338%.

00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:59.020
So what you will
be able to consume

00:36:59.020 --> 00:37:01.900
per person on the
average in 2000

00:37:01.900 --> 00:37:07.480
really depends in a big
way on a percent here

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:09.700
and a percent there
in the annual rate

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:12.160
of productivity increase.

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:17.769
Will the good fortune of
the last few years continue?

00:37:17.769 --> 00:37:18.310
I don't know.

00:37:18.310 --> 00:37:21.290
Extrapolating from four or
five years of observation

00:37:21.290 --> 00:37:25.750
to 50 years is not a
comfortable activity.

00:37:25.750 --> 00:37:27.800
It helps if you can
look a little deeper.

00:37:27.800 --> 00:37:29.780
We can look a little deeper.

00:37:29.780 --> 00:37:35.410
But the observational
basis is still very slim.

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:37.690
Mark Twain said in Life
on the Mississippi,

00:37:37.690 --> 00:37:40.240
"Isn't science wonderful?

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:43.115
You get such a lot of conjecture
on such a small allowance

00:37:43.115 --> 00:37:43.615
of fact."

00:37:46.120 --> 00:37:51.670
And we don't-- we're lacking a
good empirical basis for trying

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:55.090
to extrapolate
productivity increase.

00:37:55.090 --> 00:38:01.000
One serious investigation
I know shows that outside

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:04.030
of durable goods manufacturing,
outside of that part

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:07.870
of the economy that that
makes durable goods,

00:38:07.870 --> 00:38:12.350
there has been practically
no technological,

00:38:12.350 --> 00:38:15.760
no improvement in
pure productivity

00:38:15.760 --> 00:38:17.170
during the past five years.

00:38:17.170 --> 00:38:19.750
And interestingly
enough, about 80%

00:38:19.750 --> 00:38:23.590
of the investment in
information technology

00:38:23.590 --> 00:38:27.260
goes outside of durable
goods manufacturing.

00:38:30.850 --> 00:38:35.450
Outside of that part
of manufacturing,

00:38:35.450 --> 00:38:38.530
there's very small increment
of productivity growth.

00:38:38.530 --> 00:38:42.310
And it is over accounted
for by the sheer capital

00:38:42.310 --> 00:38:45.130
investment and improved
educational status

00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:45.920
of the workforce.

00:38:45.920 --> 00:38:48.310
So there's not much
left to be accounted

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:53.680
for by improved technology.

00:38:57.810 --> 00:39:00.420
This suggests to
me that we will be

00:39:00.420 --> 00:39:05.400
lucky to achieve the same
gains in the standard of living

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:10.020
in the next 50 years that
we did in the past 50 years.

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:12.600
We would need faster
productivity growth

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:18.030
than in the past 50 years to
offset the drag from aging.

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:21.840
I think there's a chance
that we'll get it.

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:25.570
But there is really
no certainty,

00:39:25.570 --> 00:39:29.880
nothing remotely like certainty,
that we will get a lot more.

00:39:29.880 --> 00:39:32.880
What this suggests
to me is that we

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:36.600
need to pursue technological
improvement, not

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:42.591
merely in information
technology, but in other fields

00:39:42.591 --> 00:39:43.090
as well.

00:39:43.090 --> 00:39:45.600
It might be a good idea
if somebody majored in

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:49.523
something other than the
EECS once in a while.

00:39:49.523 --> 00:39:55.240
[CHEERING]

00:39:55.240 --> 00:39:58.000
That still leaves
us with inequality

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:00.130
and the problem of the
unskilled and uneducated.

00:40:00.130 --> 00:40:04.420
Even suppose that we can manage
the acceleration productivity

00:40:04.420 --> 00:40:10.310
to offset the drag that comes
from an older population,

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:13.600
we would still have
that problem of dealing

00:40:13.600 --> 00:40:17.200
with the unskilled
and uneducated.

00:40:17.200 --> 00:40:19.900
The next 50 years
appear, if it is

00:40:19.900 --> 00:40:23.140
a period of rapid
technological innovation,

00:40:23.140 --> 00:40:26.980
could make that
problem worse unless we

00:40:26.980 --> 00:40:31.300
find some way of tackling the
problems of the bottom third

00:40:31.300 --> 00:40:33.650
of the income distribution.

00:40:33.650 --> 00:40:37.270
It's not clear that we have
either ideas, new ideas

00:40:37.270 --> 00:40:44.560
or any willingness to
do something about that.

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:47.290
And I will leave it there
and turn the floor over

00:40:47.290 --> 00:40:51.177
to my colleague Modigliani.

00:40:51.177 --> 00:40:55.153
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:19.070
MODIGLIANI: When I was
asked to join Paul and Bob

00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:26.160
on this occasion, I immediately
thought or remembered

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:33.590
a famous line from my great poet
Dante Alighieri, the great poet

00:41:33.590 --> 00:41:39.890
of Italy, who tells how
he got into the beginning

00:41:39.890 --> 00:41:44.670
of the inferno and
there he was met--

00:41:44.670 --> 00:41:49.660
he was lost-- and he
was met by Virgil.

00:41:49.660 --> 00:41:54.470
And while they were
talking, in came Homer.

00:41:54.470 --> 00:42:00.236
And then Dante says, and I
was third among such genius.

00:42:00.236 --> 00:42:02.620
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:02.620 --> 00:42:04.871
I hope I can keep it up.

00:42:04.871 --> 00:42:05.370
Let's see.

00:42:09.080 --> 00:42:14.670
First of all, let me say that I
had the privilege of suggesting

00:42:14.670 --> 00:42:17.620
the title for tonight
because I thought, and I

00:42:17.620 --> 00:42:20.080
wanted to be sure, everybody
would be interested.

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:24.340
Now, everybody here must
be either interested

00:42:24.340 --> 00:42:27.010
or have lived the
last 50 years or must

00:42:27.010 --> 00:42:29.330
expect to live in the next 50.

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:33.830
So everybody should be involved.

00:42:33.830 --> 00:42:38.410
Next, let me say
that in general, I

00:42:38.410 --> 00:42:42.940
have developed ideas that
are similar to those of Bob,

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:47.390
and I generally agree with
his analysis his conclusions.

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:51.940
So let me stress the few points
where I have something to add.

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:54.110
And I say they
are not that many.

00:42:56.890 --> 00:43:00.850
One thing is that,
I want to point out,

00:43:00.850 --> 00:43:05.380
we have seen that the last 50
years have been exceptional

00:43:05.380 --> 00:43:12.810
in terms of growth, have seen
people get a lot better off,

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:14.860
a lot richer.

00:43:14.860 --> 00:43:19.210
I want to point out
that, in effect, there

00:43:19.210 --> 00:43:22.970
is a group of people
that did even better.

00:43:22.970 --> 00:43:25.900
And that is our group.

00:43:25.900 --> 00:43:29.020
We were particularly
fortunate because we

00:43:29.020 --> 00:43:33.100
had in addition to this
growth and productivity,

00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:36.760
we had three things
working for us.

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:41.070
First, a stock
market boom, we find

00:43:41.070 --> 00:43:43.110
that our wealth
has been multiplied

00:43:43.110 --> 00:43:44.280
without our doing anything.

00:43:48.000 --> 00:43:52.950
I never expected that I
would have so much wealth

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:54.730
as I end up having.

00:43:54.730 --> 00:43:58.610
It's completely beyond
my wildest thoughts.

00:44:01.230 --> 00:44:04.320
Whatever I didn't put or we
didn't put in the stock market,

00:44:04.320 --> 00:44:06.230
we put into real estate.

00:44:06.230 --> 00:44:12.560
And that is just as much,
again, pure luck as not.

00:44:12.560 --> 00:44:14.863
Now, there is a
slight difference

00:44:14.863 --> 00:44:18.410
that they, on the
whole, the real estate

00:44:18.410 --> 00:44:22.740
has a reason for rising
because land is fixed

00:44:22.740 --> 00:44:26.120
and population is expanding
and income is expanding.

00:44:26.120 --> 00:44:29.700
Therefore, the value of land,
particularly urban land,

00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:30.690
must tend to rise.

00:44:30.690 --> 00:44:31.816
There is a good reason.

00:44:31.816 --> 00:44:33.690
In the case of the stock
market, there really

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:35.140
isn't any good reason.

00:44:35.140 --> 00:44:39.120
And I think and I
have said many times

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:42.600
that much of what is going
on in the stock market

00:44:42.600 --> 00:44:47.880
reflects a bubble, not
entirely, not entirely.

00:44:47.880 --> 00:44:50.200
There is-- let me
put it this way.

00:44:50.200 --> 00:44:56.800
The price of a share can be
seen as depending on the profit

00:44:56.800 --> 00:45:01.860
it earns multiplied by
the profit earnings ratio,

00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:02.830
how much--

00:45:02.830 --> 00:45:05.430
and price earning ratio,
what is the price per dollar.

00:45:05.430 --> 00:45:09.480
So dollar earned times
price earnings gives you

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:11.640
the price of the share.

00:45:11.640 --> 00:45:14.730
Now, what has happened
in this period

00:45:14.730 --> 00:45:18.600
is that both the earnings
have grown rapidly

00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:21.790
and the price
earnings have gone up.

00:45:21.790 --> 00:45:26.770
And the growth of the earnings
is partly a reflection

00:45:26.770 --> 00:45:29.770
of this very sound economy.

00:45:29.770 --> 00:45:32.050
It's a reflection of the
productivity, a reflection

00:45:32.050 --> 00:45:40.000
of many things and rather
acquiescent labor behavior,

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:43.430
which is sort of
allowed on this level.

00:45:43.430 --> 00:45:46.090
But the rise in the
price earning ratio,

00:45:46.090 --> 00:45:48.990
which keeps going on,
is not of this kind.

00:45:48.990 --> 00:45:50.700
That is the bubble.

00:45:50.700 --> 00:45:54.690
There is no reason why as
long as the earnings grow

00:45:54.690 --> 00:45:57.810
at a constant rate, there is no
reason why is the price earning

00:45:57.810 --> 00:45:59.340
ratio should rise.

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:02.430
If it rises, it is
because of the bubble.

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:03.490
And what's a bubble?

00:46:03.490 --> 00:46:08.690
It's a situation where a price,
a ridiculously high price

00:46:08.690 --> 00:46:11.990
is justified by the
fact that everybody

00:46:11.990 --> 00:46:15.810
expects that the next price
next year will be even higher.

00:46:15.810 --> 00:46:20.030
So no matter how ridiculous it
is, as long as it keeps rising,

00:46:20.030 --> 00:46:21.230
it's justified.

00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:24.500
And this, by the way, one
learns from Paul's work

00:46:24.500 --> 00:46:26.030
to some extent.

00:46:26.030 --> 00:46:33.200
And the trouble is that as--

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:36.950
if the process continues and
the price continues to rise,

00:46:36.950 --> 00:46:39.640
it justifies the
price, but the price

00:46:39.640 --> 00:46:40.890
gets more and more ridiculous.

00:46:40.890 --> 00:46:45.920
It gets more and more
out of line with reality.

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:51.080
And we economists always of
think of a famous example,

00:46:51.080 --> 00:46:55.970
the so-called tulip
bulb boom or bubble

00:46:55.970 --> 00:46:58.670
where tulips suddenly--
where the price of tulips

00:46:58.670 --> 00:47:01.790
went up many, many
hundreds of times.

00:47:01.790 --> 00:47:04.370
And there was no good reason.

00:47:04.370 --> 00:47:07.260
Eventually the thing collapsed
because at some point,

00:47:07.260 --> 00:47:10.040
people think the price
is ridiculously high.

00:47:10.040 --> 00:47:12.410
And they do-- I'm not
willing to bid it any higher.

00:47:12.410 --> 00:47:13.640
And then it stops growing.

00:47:13.640 --> 00:47:17.780
When it stops growing, it
cannot hold, collapses.

00:47:17.780 --> 00:47:22.680
So I think that bubble
has been-- made us rich

00:47:22.680 --> 00:47:25.490
and may present
a problem for you

00:47:25.490 --> 00:47:28.430
from now on into the future.

00:47:28.430 --> 00:47:30.360
Because I think at
some point, there

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:34.580
is going to be a realignment.

00:47:34.580 --> 00:47:39.790
The case to some extent
will be true in real estate.

00:47:39.790 --> 00:47:45.740
And there the piece of luck
that we have had completely

00:47:45.740 --> 00:47:48.930
destroyed is Medicare.

00:47:48.930 --> 00:47:53.160
Medicare is being paid
by the young for the old.

00:47:53.160 --> 00:47:55.100
But when we were
young, we didn't pay

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:57.472
because Medicare didn't exist.

00:47:57.472 --> 00:47:59.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:59.280 --> 00:48:02.330
So you are paying for
us on the expectation

00:48:02.330 --> 00:48:03.800
that somebody will pay for you.

00:48:03.800 --> 00:48:07.820
If we never paid anything and
we got that incredible boom

00:48:07.820 --> 00:48:12.260
just as we were getting old
and in the need of Medicare.

00:48:12.260 --> 00:48:17.230
So these three things are
very hard to reproduce.

00:48:17.230 --> 00:48:28.440
Now, however, we
have done so well.

00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:32.170
And some of this, as I
say, is not reproducible.

00:48:32.170 --> 00:48:35.250
So I agree with Bob
that you are not

00:48:35.250 --> 00:48:39.690
likely to do quite as well,
no matter what, quite as well

00:48:39.690 --> 00:48:41.972
as some of us has done.

00:48:41.972 --> 00:48:46.410
Now, of course, because of
we are unexpectedly rich,

00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:49.710
we may be leaving you young
people larger inheritance

00:48:49.710 --> 00:48:51.490
than we might have
done otherwise.

00:48:51.490 --> 00:48:53.320
And that will help
you to some extent,

00:48:53.320 --> 00:48:57.120
will carry over,
but not completely.

00:48:57.120 --> 00:49:04.030
Now we have to have that
this great development

00:49:04.030 --> 00:49:09.400
in this country still
is somewhat unique.

00:49:09.400 --> 00:49:11.820
Because the other
major competitor,

00:49:11.820 --> 00:49:14.440
the other part of
the world that has

00:49:14.440 --> 00:49:19.360
similar capacities, similar
qualities of labor, capital, et

00:49:19.360 --> 00:49:22.480
cetera is Europe.

00:49:22.480 --> 00:49:25.900
And Europe is not
doing anywhere as well.

00:49:25.900 --> 00:49:28.090
And why aren't
they doing as well?

00:49:28.090 --> 00:49:31.060
Well, first of all,
objectively, Europe

00:49:31.060 --> 00:49:37.400
still has something like
10%, 11% unemployment rate,

00:49:37.400 --> 00:49:39.070
average unemployment rate.

00:49:39.070 --> 00:49:42.200
It depends, it varies from
one country to another.

00:49:42.200 --> 00:49:46.720
But their average is still 11%.

00:49:46.720 --> 00:49:50.090
And furthermore, the
way they are moving,

00:49:50.090 --> 00:49:54.780
they're not likely to
cut that down quickly.

00:49:54.780 --> 00:49:57.000
They are very happy
when they grow 3%.

00:49:57.000 --> 00:50:00.600
And 3% is not much to cut
into the unemployment,

00:50:00.600 --> 00:50:04.170
just enough to absorb the
productivity and population

00:50:04.170 --> 00:50:05.740
growth.

00:50:05.740 --> 00:50:08.112
Now, why are they
doing so badly?

00:50:08.112 --> 00:50:09.320
Well, I think the answer is--

00:50:09.320 --> 00:50:11.160
Paul gave it before--

00:50:11.160 --> 00:50:13.920
because they do not read Keynes.

00:50:13.920 --> 00:50:17.710
They refuse to
understand Keynes.

00:50:17.710 --> 00:50:19.980
They are stupid enough
so that they will not

00:50:19.980 --> 00:50:22.170
read and understand
that if there is not

00:50:22.170 --> 00:50:25.313
enough money in the economy,
you have unemployment.

00:50:25.313 --> 00:50:29.970
OK, so they keep a
policy which is wrong.

00:50:29.970 --> 00:50:34.050
And they keep it continuously
even to the present time.

00:50:34.050 --> 00:50:35.910
You just had an example.

00:50:35.910 --> 00:50:39.420
With 10% unemployment,
the central bank

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:42.780
raises interest rates
without understanding

00:50:42.780 --> 00:50:46.110
that the way to cut unemployment
is the American way--

00:50:46.110 --> 00:50:48.570
invest, invest, invest.

00:50:48.570 --> 00:50:50.560
That's why we are doing so well.

00:50:50.560 --> 00:50:53.300
That's why they are
doing so poorly.

00:50:53.300 --> 00:50:56.240
That's, by the way,
exactly what Keynes said.

00:50:56.240 --> 00:51:00.370
Investment is what
determines income, OK?

00:51:00.370 --> 00:51:02.690
That's the major determiner.

00:51:02.690 --> 00:51:05.250
Now, it can be bailed out
by government or private

00:51:05.250 --> 00:51:07.070
here in this
country, fortunately,

00:51:07.070 --> 00:51:08.910
it's mostly private investment.

00:51:08.910 --> 00:51:09.810
That's what I think.

00:51:09.810 --> 00:51:13.020
But that is what is
driving us so high.

00:51:13.020 --> 00:51:16.260
And that's in the way of Europe.

00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:18.760
I do hope that at some point,
they will learn Keynes.

00:51:18.760 --> 00:51:19.670
There still is hope.

00:51:19.670 --> 00:51:22.190
I have been fighting my way.

00:51:22.190 --> 00:51:24.720
But I have just--

00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:28.100
a couple of months ago, I
wrote a paper explaining this.

00:51:28.100 --> 00:51:31.970
And one of my colleagues said,
poor Modigliani, he still

00:51:31.970 --> 00:51:34.246
believes in Keynes.

00:51:34.246 --> 00:51:37.310
That's the European mentality.

00:51:37.310 --> 00:51:41.514
To which I replied, poor guy,
you never understood Keynes.

00:51:41.514 --> 00:51:43.999
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:45.490 --> 00:51:56.940
Now, as we look ahead
into the future,

00:51:56.940 --> 00:51:59.820
aside from these
known advantages,

00:51:59.820 --> 00:52:04.430
there are some definite
problems to worry about.

00:52:04.430 --> 00:52:12.310
And I would like
to mention some.

00:52:12.310 --> 00:52:17.590
First of all, there is the
fact of the Wall Street bubble,

00:52:17.590 --> 00:52:22.860
that when the bubble
explodes, as I expect,

00:52:22.860 --> 00:52:28.720
there will be unpleasant
consequences in the economy.

00:52:28.720 --> 00:52:33.250
There will be very likely
some decrease in demand

00:52:33.250 --> 00:52:36.030
because consumption
will decrease.

00:52:36.030 --> 00:52:40.020
You keep reading everywhere
about the wealth effect.

00:52:40.020 --> 00:52:44.100
Consumption is so high
because of the wealth effect.

00:52:44.100 --> 00:52:47.670
You seldom see it,
footnote it says, that is

00:52:47.670 --> 00:52:52.110
to be found for the first time
in Modigliani's life-cycle.

00:52:52.110 --> 00:52:54.670
That's where the
wealth effect was first

00:52:54.670 --> 00:52:57.820
mentioned in the 1950s.

00:52:57.820 --> 00:52:59.520
Now it's become very popular.

00:52:59.520 --> 00:53:02.450
But that effect
certainly, I'm sure,

00:53:02.450 --> 00:53:05.640
I believe that that is
an important thing that

00:53:05.640 --> 00:53:13.050
has supported our boom, although
more than supporting our boom,

00:53:13.050 --> 00:53:15.860
it has done something
that's not so good,

00:53:15.860 --> 00:53:21.420
creating a large deficit
in the balance of payment.

00:53:21.420 --> 00:53:24.000
Remember that that is a problem.

00:53:24.000 --> 00:53:27.220
We have a gigantic debt.

00:53:27.220 --> 00:53:32.360
We have a gigantic deficit.

00:53:32.360 --> 00:53:35.630
That is, we spent
more than we earned.

00:53:35.630 --> 00:53:40.300
We used more resources
than we produced, OK?

00:53:40.300 --> 00:53:44.060
Now, one has to be
careful in distinguishing

00:53:44.060 --> 00:53:48.730
whether having a
deficit or being in debt

00:53:48.730 --> 00:53:51.865
is bad, per se, from the size.

00:53:51.865 --> 00:53:57.640
The fact of being in debt is
not, per se, bad or in deficit.

00:53:57.640 --> 00:54:01.750
Think of anybody like
you in your age group.

00:54:01.750 --> 00:54:04.660
You are maybe who will
be buying a house.

00:54:04.660 --> 00:54:08.410
In the year in which you buy
a house, your income is $1,000

00:54:08.410 --> 00:54:10.810
and you spend $20,000.

00:54:10.810 --> 00:54:14.380
So you have your terrific
deficit and you go into debt.

00:54:14.380 --> 00:54:15.010
Is that bad?

00:54:15.010 --> 00:54:15.670
No.

00:54:15.670 --> 00:54:19.210
Because against the
debt, you have an asset.

00:54:19.210 --> 00:54:21.850
The same thing is
true in this country.

00:54:21.850 --> 00:54:22.630
We borrow.

00:54:22.630 --> 00:54:25.120
But we borrow to invest.

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:28.150
So essentially,
against the debt,

00:54:28.150 --> 00:54:31.120
we do have earning assets.

00:54:31.120 --> 00:54:34.540
And there is nothing per
se wrong with borrowing.

00:54:34.540 --> 00:54:38.510
The problem is that
if you go too far then

00:54:38.510 --> 00:54:42.420
the creditors may begin
to get worried, OK?

00:54:42.420 --> 00:54:45.030
Yeah, you're investing, but
look at how big your debt is.

00:54:45.030 --> 00:54:48.470
If something goes wrong,
you will be unable to pay.

00:54:48.470 --> 00:54:55.030
And that's the danger we face
in our balance of payment,

00:54:55.030 --> 00:54:58.640
that it is conceivable
that at some point

00:54:58.640 --> 00:55:03.800
foreigners will refuse
to continue to lend us

00:55:03.800 --> 00:55:06.770
or to buy the shares
of our companies,

00:55:06.770 --> 00:55:10.840
to provide the capital,
because they've had enough.

00:55:10.840 --> 00:55:16.720
And if that happens and if they
want to stop bringing in money

00:55:16.720 --> 00:55:20.470
and the dollar might
begin to stop appreciating

00:55:20.470 --> 00:55:23.920
and might begin to depreciate,
then they may be wrong.

00:55:23.920 --> 00:55:25.780
Everybody may want to
get out of the dollar

00:55:25.780 --> 00:55:27.890
before it depreciates further.

00:55:27.890 --> 00:55:31.960
And then the United States
is no different from Malaysia

00:55:31.960 --> 00:55:34.280
or from Singapore--
no, Singapore

00:55:34.280 --> 00:55:37.270
did all right, but any of
the states of the governments

00:55:37.270 --> 00:55:40.720
that appear to be in
complete disarray.

00:55:40.720 --> 00:55:46.030
Because with further changes,
if people want to get out,

00:55:46.030 --> 00:55:47.680
there is nothing
you can do except

00:55:47.680 --> 00:55:49.600
let the currency devalue.

00:55:49.600 --> 00:55:51.892
That's the only-- you
have to let it go.

00:55:51.892 --> 00:55:53.475
When that happens,
you have inflation.

00:55:53.475 --> 00:55:55.780
When you have inflation,
the central bank

00:55:55.780 --> 00:55:58.330
will raise interest
rates like [INAUDIBLE]..

00:55:58.330 --> 00:56:00.872
Investment will decline.

00:56:00.872 --> 00:56:06.440
And we could have
serious consequences,

00:56:06.440 --> 00:56:11.380
including contributing to
the stock market crash.

00:56:11.380 --> 00:56:14.510
Because as people get out,
they want to sell the shares

00:56:14.510 --> 00:56:16.870
they own and have
additional pressure.

00:56:16.870 --> 00:56:20.650
So that complex of very high--

00:56:20.650 --> 00:56:23.870
I mean very high-- stock
prices bubble, the deficit

00:56:23.870 --> 00:56:27.130
and the balance of
payment is a complex that

00:56:27.130 --> 00:56:32.980
could generate some serious
problems in the future.

00:56:32.980 --> 00:56:34.420
It need not.

00:56:34.420 --> 00:56:37.459
That is, it is
possible that things

00:56:37.459 --> 00:56:38.500
would sort of quiet down.

00:56:38.500 --> 00:56:43.180
But the main way in which
we could help the situation

00:56:43.180 --> 00:56:45.130
would be by saving more.

00:56:45.130 --> 00:56:49.630
Because if we save more, we
can serve those investment,

00:56:49.630 --> 00:56:53.320
we can finance those
investments without the need

00:56:53.320 --> 00:56:55.480
to borrow abroad.

00:56:55.480 --> 00:56:59.870
So that would be a
very desirable thing.

00:56:59.870 --> 00:57:05.900
Now, let me come to another
problem which I think

00:57:05.900 --> 00:57:09.320
is a part of the long-run view.

00:57:09.320 --> 00:57:12.470
And that is what
Bob has mentioned,

00:57:12.470 --> 00:57:16.560
the problem of Social Security.

00:57:16.560 --> 00:57:24.270
We do know that we have a
system of Social Security which

00:57:24.270 --> 00:57:32.220
is called pay as you go, a
system in which the pensions

00:57:32.220 --> 00:57:35.220
are not paid out
of the accumulation

00:57:35.220 --> 00:57:36.240
that you have made.

00:57:36.240 --> 00:57:38.640
See, in the normal system,
or in my life cycle,

00:57:38.640 --> 00:57:40.380
people accumulate wealth.

00:57:40.380 --> 00:57:44.190
And they use that
wealth to live when

00:57:44.190 --> 00:57:48.150
they are old, OK, to
essentially pay their pension.

00:57:48.150 --> 00:57:53.600
The present system is instead,
you do not build up a wealth.

00:57:53.600 --> 00:57:56.820
You take the money that
is contributed by people.

00:57:56.820 --> 00:58:01.010
And instead of putting it aside,
you use it to pay pensions.

00:58:01.010 --> 00:58:04.360
So there is no accumulation.

00:58:04.360 --> 00:58:09.130
Now, that has
several consequences.

00:58:09.130 --> 00:58:15.800
One consequence is that
the system is unstable

00:58:15.800 --> 00:58:22.580
because the relation between
contribution and pensions

00:58:22.580 --> 00:58:26.360
depends on how fast
the economy grows.

00:58:26.360 --> 00:58:29.510
If the economy
goes fast, then you

00:58:29.510 --> 00:58:34.220
can get out more than you put
in because there are more people

00:58:34.220 --> 00:58:37.850
to pay with their
contribution then

00:58:37.850 --> 00:58:39.560
there were when you started.

00:58:39.560 --> 00:58:43.110
So you could get more money
than you could put in.

00:58:43.110 --> 00:58:46.280
But if you are promised
a certain return,

00:58:46.280 --> 00:58:48.370
that's like an
implicit interest rate,

00:58:48.370 --> 00:58:51.290
if you promise a certain return.

00:58:51.290 --> 00:58:54.770
And if the growth changes,
then you cannot deliver

00:58:54.770 --> 00:58:56.300
the promises.

00:58:56.300 --> 00:58:59.480
And what's happened in this
country and throughout most

00:58:59.480 --> 00:59:04.160
of the world is that as
population growth declines,

00:59:04.160 --> 00:59:08.160
you cannot fulfill those
promises because the ratio

00:59:08.160 --> 00:59:13.190
of people that pay contribution
to those who get pensions

00:59:13.190 --> 00:59:14.610
changes and deteriorates.

00:59:14.610 --> 00:59:17.070
You have less and less
people paying per person

00:59:17.070 --> 00:59:19.460
taking a pension.

00:59:19.460 --> 00:59:22.400
That is sure to create problems.

00:59:22.400 --> 00:59:27.900
Now, how much and how bad will
depend on what we do about it.

00:59:27.900 --> 00:59:33.560
The present administration
has no program to handle that.

00:59:33.560 --> 00:59:39.830
That is, it will put in, throw
in the surplus of the budget

00:59:39.830 --> 00:59:41.960
to support Social Security.

00:59:41.960 --> 00:59:45.530
But that will not get you beyond
the middle of the next century.

00:59:45.530 --> 00:59:48.290
By that time,
approximately, there

00:59:48.290 --> 00:59:55.190
will have to be something like
a 50% increase in the dues

00:59:55.190 --> 01:00:02.530
in the what you have to pay
in the payroll tax or a cut

01:00:02.530 --> 01:00:05.060
in benefits.

01:00:05.060 --> 01:00:10.820
And here is the point where
I have something to offer.

01:00:10.820 --> 01:00:14.590
I've been working now
for a couple of years

01:00:14.590 --> 01:00:17.600
on finding a solution
to this problem

01:00:17.600 --> 01:00:20.240
and find that the
solution exists

01:00:20.240 --> 01:00:23.130
and it's really rather simple.

01:00:23.130 --> 01:00:26.690
The solution
consists in creating

01:00:26.690 --> 01:00:29.220
a so-called funded system.

01:00:29.220 --> 01:00:33.740
A funded system is one in which
the contributions, instead

01:00:33.740 --> 01:00:39.220
of being used to pay pensions,
are used to accumulate wealth.

01:00:39.220 --> 01:00:42.610
And then when you retire,
that wealth and not

01:00:42.610 --> 01:00:46.330
the young people, but that
wealth pays your pension.

01:00:46.330 --> 01:00:50.020
Now, once you have been
able, you've made the change,

01:00:50.020 --> 01:00:53.410
you can see that this
system is far better.

01:00:53.410 --> 01:00:57.400
Because in the first
place, it is cheaper.

01:00:57.400 --> 01:01:03.160
We have figured out that under
the pay as you go, by 2050

01:01:03.160 --> 01:01:06.640
or thereafter, you will
have to pay about 20%,

01:01:06.640 --> 01:01:11.260
21% contribution instead
of the 12% of today.

01:01:11.260 --> 01:01:14.590
With our system, you
would pay only about 7%.

01:01:14.590 --> 01:01:18.430
So it would be able to
cut, get over the crisis,

01:01:18.430 --> 01:01:21.490
and cut contributions
very substantially,

01:01:21.490 --> 01:01:25.310
by about a third.

01:01:25.310 --> 01:01:32.380
And furthermore, a funded system
is less sensitive to variations

01:01:32.380 --> 01:01:35.200
in growth.

01:01:35.200 --> 01:01:40.150
It's completely, well, largely
unaffected by population growth

01:01:40.150 --> 01:01:42.550
because population affects
the picture because they

01:01:42.550 --> 01:01:44.740
are young support the old.

01:01:44.740 --> 01:01:47.320
When the old are supported
by their capital,

01:01:47.320 --> 01:01:51.030
they don't care how many
young people there are.

01:01:51.030 --> 01:01:54.050
Now, you have to
understand the moment,

01:01:54.050 --> 01:01:58.920
how that fits into the
picture that Bob has given

01:01:58.920 --> 01:02:01.450
and which is very
commonly accepted.

01:02:01.450 --> 01:02:09.000
The idea is that, how can you
avoid getting poorer or paying

01:02:09.000 --> 01:02:15.710
less pensions if you
have less people, more

01:02:15.710 --> 01:02:19.310
old people to supply?

01:02:19.310 --> 01:02:22.280
Because, he says,
that's the pie.

01:02:22.280 --> 01:02:26.700
If you've got more for them,
you've got less for the others.

01:02:26.700 --> 01:02:29.510
Well, the answer is
that under our system,

01:02:29.510 --> 01:02:32.360
the pie is not fixed.

01:02:32.360 --> 01:02:38.360
The pie precisely
depends on what you need.

01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:42.310
If you have many old people,
you will have large accumulation

01:02:42.310 --> 01:02:46.040
from the past, which
will take care of it.

01:02:46.040 --> 01:02:51.170
They may be to the point where
the accumulation, the fund,

01:02:51.170 --> 01:02:55.040
will be decreasing because
they'll pay out than it gets.

01:02:55.040 --> 01:02:56.480
But that's all right.

01:02:56.480 --> 01:02:58.010
Paying out more
than it gets simply

01:02:58.010 --> 01:03:01.000
means that you would be
liquidating some assets.

01:03:01.000 --> 01:03:03.570
Society as a whole,
which is not growing,

01:03:03.570 --> 01:03:05.510
would need less assets anyway.

01:03:05.510 --> 01:03:08.370
So the thing would largely
take care of itself.

01:03:08.370 --> 01:03:16.170
So this is, I think, a kind of
possibility that gives us hope.

01:03:16.170 --> 01:03:19.530
I mean, obviously, I don't
have time to go into details.

01:03:19.530 --> 01:03:24.620
But I should have brought
with me the website

01:03:24.620 --> 01:03:25.730
where you can find this.

01:03:25.730 --> 01:03:29.530
By the way, I can tell you,
if you look in the website

01:03:29.530 --> 01:03:33.200
under MIT and under
Franco Modigliani,

01:03:33.200 --> 01:03:35.210
you will find
there that there is

01:03:35.210 --> 01:03:38.540
the latest version of my plan.

01:03:38.540 --> 01:03:39.876
Thank you.

01:03:39.876 --> 01:03:44.313
[APPLAUSE]

01:03:58.757 --> 01:03:59.340
SOLOW: Thanks.

01:03:59.340 --> 01:04:01.830
There are a couple of
microphones set up,

01:04:01.830 --> 01:04:03.944
at least in that aisle
and in that aisle.

01:04:03.944 --> 01:04:04.860
I can't see if there--

01:04:04.860 --> 01:04:07.710
I think those are
all that there is.

01:04:07.710 --> 01:04:15.690
But we're happy to have some
conversation with anyone who

01:04:15.690 --> 01:04:21.000
has a question or a
pronouncement or a theorem.

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:22.190
Who knows?

01:04:22.190 --> 01:04:25.010
AUDIENCE: You'd have to be
very brave for a pronouncement

01:04:25.010 --> 01:04:26.590
in this group.

01:04:26.590 --> 01:04:29.700
A question to all
of the panel has

01:04:29.700 --> 01:04:35.940
to do with aging and also
your solution, Dr. Modigliani.

01:04:35.940 --> 01:04:40.740
It seems as if you're making an
assumption that the aging will

01:04:40.740 --> 01:04:45.480
have an effect which is bad.

01:04:45.480 --> 01:04:49.710
You were mentioning that
retirement age is going down

01:04:49.710 --> 01:04:50.830
rather than up.

01:04:50.830 --> 01:04:53.820
You know, but look
at the three of you.

01:04:53.820 --> 01:04:57.360
In many parts of the
United States at least,

01:04:57.360 --> 01:05:01.530
people may have retired, but
they may also still be working.

01:05:01.530 --> 01:05:03.960
The fact of people
being, of older people

01:05:03.960 --> 01:05:07.162
being healthier and healthier,
I wonder it's a really--

01:05:07.162 --> 01:05:08.620
and I just don't
know if, you know,

01:05:08.620 --> 01:05:10.800
I'm just asking
because I don't know.

01:05:10.800 --> 01:05:12.720
Has there been any
work done to really try

01:05:12.720 --> 01:05:15.830
to quantify that aging effect?

01:05:15.830 --> 01:05:17.640
And then I'll look
at your website

01:05:17.640 --> 01:05:19.990
for the second question.

01:05:19.990 --> 01:05:22.770
SOLOW: Well, let me--

01:05:22.770 --> 01:05:26.310
I think there is a
little illusion here

01:05:26.310 --> 01:05:30.870
that is shared always
by people like us.

01:05:30.870 --> 01:05:33.890
By us I mean everyone
here in this room.

01:05:33.890 --> 01:05:36.090
We're people who on the whole--

01:05:36.090 --> 01:05:38.220
well, let me back
up a minute and say,

01:05:38.220 --> 01:05:42.930
one obvious way for
a society to deal

01:05:42.930 --> 01:05:47.340
with this aging
fact, demography,

01:05:47.340 --> 01:05:50.970
is to prolong the working life.

01:05:50.970 --> 01:05:57.630
Just as was just said,
we are living longer.

01:05:57.630 --> 01:06:02.040
We're living-- we're
having healthier old age.

01:06:02.040 --> 01:06:05.223
Why should not the
working life be prolonged?

01:06:08.470 --> 01:06:15.280
People like us who like our
work, who rather don't like

01:06:15.280 --> 01:06:19.150
the notion of retiring, we
want to hang on to the office

01:06:19.150 --> 01:06:25.510
and continue to work, that seems
a little more obvious than it

01:06:25.510 --> 01:06:26.740
is.

01:06:26.740 --> 01:06:31.510
The society is full of
people who do dull work

01:06:31.510 --> 01:06:33.250
and don't like it.

01:06:33.250 --> 01:06:35.670
And they will not
be happy to be told

01:06:35.670 --> 01:06:39.010
that, well, the way
we deal with this

01:06:39.010 --> 01:06:45.700
is for you instead of retiring
at 65 or more likely 62 or 61

01:06:45.700 --> 01:06:51.820
as you had planned, you will, of
course, use your healthy years

01:06:51.820 --> 01:06:55.120
being a clerk in a department
store for another five or 10

01:06:55.120 --> 01:07:02.980
years or for doing one or
another kind of work that's

01:07:02.980 --> 01:07:06.200
not so pleasant and
not so rewarding.

01:07:06.200 --> 01:07:08.350
I think it's
inevitable, actually,

01:07:08.350 --> 01:07:12.280
that the working life
will be prolonged

01:07:12.280 --> 01:07:15.130
in the US and elsewhere
simply because people

01:07:15.130 --> 01:07:18.430
live longer and are healthier.

01:07:18.430 --> 01:07:24.640
And the way to deal
with the productivity

01:07:24.640 --> 01:07:30.980
problem that emerges is to
prolong the age of retirement.

01:07:30.980 --> 01:07:36.190
I think the scheme that
Franco was describing,

01:07:36.190 --> 01:07:41.740
the connection between
what he was saying

01:07:41.740 --> 01:07:46.030
and what I was saying is
that this extra accumulation

01:07:46.030 --> 01:07:49.870
of capital that comes
with the funded system

01:07:49.870 --> 01:07:53.230
is precisely what gives you
the extra productivity that's

01:07:53.230 --> 01:07:54.280
needed to support--

01:07:54.280 --> 01:07:55.410
SAMUELSON: Exactly.

01:07:55.410 --> 01:07:59.240
SOLOW: --a population, a smaller
fraction of which is working.

01:07:59.240 --> 01:08:06.250
The amount of work performed
by the population measured

01:08:06.250 --> 01:08:11.590
in hours will diminish.

01:08:14.220 --> 01:08:19.640
The decreasing age of
retirement is not an illusion.

01:08:19.640 --> 01:08:22.010
For people like us, it is.

01:08:22.010 --> 01:08:27.890
But for people who work
in automobile factories

01:08:27.890 --> 01:08:32.540
and now live in RVs,
recreational vehicles

01:08:32.540 --> 01:08:36.830
in Florida, it is not the case.

01:08:36.830 --> 01:08:40.160
So something like the
extra capital accumulation

01:08:40.160 --> 01:08:40.840
is what we need.

01:08:40.840 --> 01:08:42.546
Franco, did you want to
add something to that.

01:08:42.546 --> 01:08:44.295
MODIGLIANI: Well, I'll
just add one thing.

01:08:44.295 --> 01:08:48.410
First of all that every
thinking person agrees

01:08:48.410 --> 01:08:50.660
that the least you
want to do is you

01:08:50.660 --> 01:08:53.660
want to give incentives
to work longer.

01:08:53.660 --> 01:08:55.670
Whether or not you
make it mandatory,

01:08:55.670 --> 01:08:57.779
you want to give incentives.

01:08:57.779 --> 01:08:59.300
And that's not an obvious thing.

01:08:59.300 --> 01:09:03.770
I mean, let me remind you
of the example of the folly

01:09:03.770 --> 01:09:07.859
of my former
country, Italy, where

01:09:07.859 --> 01:09:10.474
the reasoning was once you
retire, you have a pension.

01:09:10.474 --> 01:09:12.598
SOLOW: Do you want to take
a turn on this question?

01:09:12.598 --> 01:09:13.069
SAMUELSON: No.

01:09:13.069 --> 01:09:15.569
MODIGLIANI: You shouldn't work
anymore because otherwise you

01:09:15.569 --> 01:09:16.310
get richer.

01:09:16.310 --> 01:09:19.819
Therefore, if you work,
we'll tax it heavily.

01:09:19.819 --> 01:09:22.200
That's completely
the wrong reasoning.

01:09:22.200 --> 01:09:25.550
If you work, the fact
that you get richer, fine,

01:09:25.550 --> 01:09:28.460
that's your incentive
to work longer.

01:09:28.460 --> 01:09:31.490
So that's exactly we should
provide that kind of a thing.

01:09:31.490 --> 01:09:35.840
We might also have
some shift in the age,

01:09:35.840 --> 01:09:38.810
in the standard age
when you retire in order

01:09:38.810 --> 01:09:40.819
to maintain the
relation between-- see,

01:09:40.819 --> 01:09:43.340
this is particularly
important with respect

01:09:43.340 --> 01:09:45.439
to life expectancy.

01:09:45.439 --> 01:09:51.290
Because aside from the getting
older because of the smaller

01:09:51.290 --> 01:09:53.460
number of births, you
also have the fact

01:09:53.460 --> 01:09:55.400
that longevity is increasing.

01:09:55.400 --> 01:09:58.220
And longevity
increasing requires

01:09:58.220 --> 01:10:00.950
additional contributions
in some form.

01:10:00.950 --> 01:10:06.040
And one way to cut that
is by lengthening by time

01:10:06.040 --> 01:10:09.800
the retirement age
to life expectancy.

01:10:09.800 --> 01:10:13.640
SOLOW: Why don't we try
the other microphone?

01:10:13.640 --> 01:10:18.140
AUDIENCE: Professors, in his
remarks, Professor Solow talked

01:10:18.140 --> 01:10:21.110
about the success of
American economics

01:10:21.110 --> 01:10:25.550
and the sustainability of
rapid growth and productivity.

01:10:25.550 --> 01:10:27.830
This is, to me, a
global question.

01:10:27.830 --> 01:10:30.920
The model that was taught
to the word by Ford

01:10:30.920 --> 01:10:33.590
in the beginning of the century,
and by the American society

01:10:33.590 --> 01:10:35.930
in its whole, is a
model that is sustained

01:10:35.930 --> 01:10:41.190
by a very high level of
consumption of goods,

01:10:41.190 --> 01:10:44.650
personal transportation,
that is cars versus bus,

01:10:44.650 --> 01:10:49.190
and the production of
short-lasting products instead

01:10:49.190 --> 01:10:51.800
of quality products and what.

01:10:51.800 --> 01:10:53.680
America is the first
country in the world

01:10:53.680 --> 01:10:57.500
has number of cars per
capita that is a success.

01:10:57.500 --> 01:10:59.060
But even the most
polluting country

01:10:59.060 --> 01:11:02.210
in the world in terms
of the amount of energy

01:11:02.210 --> 01:11:03.945
that's used per capita.

01:11:03.945 --> 01:11:06.430
My question is now,
what will happen

01:11:06.430 --> 01:11:09.140
in the next decade,
the next 50 years when

01:11:09.140 --> 01:11:13.150
energetic resources will
be more crucial and costly

01:11:13.150 --> 01:11:16.840
and when some rapidly growing
countries China, for instance,

01:11:16.840 --> 01:11:20.870
will ask for the same
level of welfare of US?

01:11:20.870 --> 01:11:24.480
What will happen to global
economics at that point?

01:11:24.480 --> 01:11:25.795
And the second question that--

01:11:25.795 --> 01:11:26.505
[LAUGHTER]

01:11:26.505 --> 01:11:27.410
--what can be done--

01:11:27.410 --> 01:11:31.040
sorry-- what can be done to
develop a sustainable economic

01:11:31.040 --> 01:11:33.950
model in the next five years?

01:11:33.950 --> 01:11:35.720
SOLOW: Paul, you
want to tackle that?

01:11:35.720 --> 01:11:37.765
MODIGLIANI: I didn't
understand a word.

01:11:37.765 --> 01:11:41.360
SAMUELSON: Well, one
person, one question.

01:11:45.860 --> 01:11:49.310
It's obviously the
case that the US,

01:11:49.310 --> 01:11:53.060
with its early high
standard of living,

01:11:53.060 --> 01:11:59.420
has been plundering the
exhaustive and exhausting

01:11:59.420 --> 01:12:02.370
resources at a much faster rate.

01:12:02.370 --> 01:12:05.300
And if we are successful
in other parts

01:12:05.300 --> 01:12:11.060
of the world in raising levels
of standard of living to the US

01:12:11.060 --> 01:12:16.720
level, that will mean a
billion Chinese are plundering

01:12:16.720 --> 01:12:20.510
at the American rate,
a billion Indians

01:12:20.510 --> 01:12:24.280
will be plundering at that rate.

01:12:24.280 --> 01:12:29.290
Scientists will always
be finding new resources.

01:12:29.290 --> 01:12:34.210
And in the age after
nuclear, probably scientists

01:12:34.210 --> 01:12:36.100
can do a lot of
winning of the battle.

01:12:36.100 --> 01:12:39.730
But there still is the
second law of thermodynamics.

01:12:39.730 --> 01:12:42.380
And there still is
some limitation.

01:12:42.380 --> 01:12:46.156
And so it will
become very important

01:12:46.156 --> 01:12:53.290
not to have SUVs
versus efficient cars

01:12:53.290 --> 01:12:55.600
just because people want them.

01:12:55.600 --> 01:12:58.180
And this is one of those
places where you can't

01:12:58.180 --> 01:13:00.340
leave it to laissez-faire.

01:13:00.340 --> 01:13:02.470
Because if it's left
to laissez-faire

01:13:02.470 --> 01:13:06.250
and I want an SUV,
I'll have an SUV.

01:13:06.250 --> 01:13:13.650
But my freedom ends where your
nose begins as I swing my cane.

01:13:13.650 --> 01:13:14.826
And--

01:13:14.826 --> 01:13:17.360
[LAUGHTER]

01:13:17.360 --> 01:13:20.942
--we're all breathing
the same molecules.

01:13:20.942 --> 01:13:22.250
Julius Caesar died.

01:13:22.250 --> 01:13:25.300
And I'm breathing
some of his molecules.

01:13:25.300 --> 01:13:27.460
So we really are interdependent.

01:13:27.460 --> 01:13:34.990
And I think there will
have to be, in 2050, when,

01:13:34.990 --> 01:13:40.390
instead of our having 23%
of world output with only 5%

01:13:40.390 --> 01:13:43.930
of the population, we
will have something

01:13:43.930 --> 01:13:48.640
like 9% of world
output and there will

01:13:48.640 --> 01:13:50.870
be a lot more demands on it.

01:13:50.870 --> 01:13:56.590
And so this doesn't mean that
we can't use a pricing system.

01:13:56.590 --> 01:14:02.350
We actually are using a pricing
system to ration out pollution.

01:14:02.350 --> 01:14:06.987
But we can't rely only
on the pricing system.

01:14:06.987 --> 01:14:09.650
We need the common
rule of the road.

01:14:15.695 --> 01:14:17.570
AUDIENCE: Professor
Samuelson, in particular,

01:14:17.570 --> 01:14:19.350
but any of you who
would care to answer,

01:14:19.350 --> 01:14:22.280
I didn't hear any of you
speak of the interaction

01:14:22.280 --> 01:14:24.530
between, on the one hand,
globalization, which I think

01:14:24.530 --> 01:14:26.210
does bring, what you
were speaking of,

01:14:26.210 --> 01:14:28.790
Professor Samuelson, the
bigness being the enemy

01:14:28.790 --> 01:14:31.394
in some sense, and the
interplay between that

01:14:31.394 --> 01:14:32.810
and democratic
institutions, which

01:14:32.810 --> 01:14:35.870
are very much more
on a different scale

01:14:35.870 --> 01:14:40.760
and no longer related
in any direct way.

01:14:40.760 --> 01:14:44.840
SAMUELSON: I thought meeting
with students someone

01:14:44.840 --> 01:14:48.500
might bring up the
question that was bothering

01:14:48.500 --> 01:14:54.770
the mob in Seattle who were
protesting against the World

01:14:54.770 --> 01:15:02.720
Trade Organization because the
low prices at which I can buy

01:15:02.720 --> 01:15:09.730
at Wal-Mart are as low as they
are because somebody in China

01:15:09.730 --> 01:15:14.300
is making a very low wage.

01:15:14.300 --> 01:15:19.660
And this takes me back
to an election in 1962

01:15:19.660 --> 01:15:21.520
in Massachusetts.

01:15:21.520 --> 01:15:23.890
There was Professor
Stuart Hughes

01:15:23.890 --> 01:15:30.040
who was a very liberal professor
of history ran for Senate.

01:15:30.040 --> 01:15:33.754
And he ran against Teddy
Kennedy, the reactionary,

01:15:33.754 --> 01:15:36.420
right?

01:15:36.420 --> 01:15:42.300
And he was in favor,
Hughes, a friend of mine,

01:15:42.300 --> 01:15:46.480
but not a student
of mine, he was

01:15:46.480 --> 01:15:52.540
in favor of a $7 minimum
wage at that time.

01:15:52.540 --> 01:15:54.940
At that time, it was about $2.

01:15:54.940 --> 01:15:59.960
He multiplied it by a couple.

01:15:59.960 --> 01:16:05.440
And I said to him, you know,
the traffic won't bear this.

01:16:05.440 --> 01:16:08.270
We won't be competitive
with other countries.

01:16:08.270 --> 01:16:10.660
He said, you don't
understand, Samuelson.

01:16:10.660 --> 01:16:14.314
I'm for a $7 minimum wage
every place in the world.

01:16:14.314 --> 01:16:16.680
[LAUGHTER]

01:16:16.680 --> 01:16:22.310
And I said to him, that is the
cruelest remark I have heard

01:16:22.310 --> 01:16:24.910
in all of my long lifetime.

01:16:24.910 --> 01:16:31.970
Because a $7 1962
real minimum wage

01:16:31.970 --> 01:16:36.140
US purchasing power
would have caused,

01:16:36.140 --> 01:16:41.990
of the five billion people
then alive, about 4 billion

01:16:41.990 --> 01:16:46.320
to die of unemployment
and starvation.

01:16:46.320 --> 01:16:48.590
So the requisite problem--

01:16:48.590 --> 01:16:51.050
this is what makes
economists such awful people,

01:16:51.050 --> 01:16:54.500
you're even awful to
your own children.

01:16:54.500 --> 01:16:58.280
You say these practical things.

01:16:58.280 --> 01:17:04.550
What you have to ask is not do
I want a poor little girl 12

01:17:04.550 --> 01:17:08.870
years old in China to
be getting the wage

01:17:08.870 --> 01:17:13.940
that Walmart eventually
pays and, of course,

01:17:13.940 --> 01:17:20.040
the answer is almost nobody but
a few sadists would want that.

01:17:20.040 --> 01:17:25.970
But what are the alternatives
if you don't have globalization.

01:17:25.970 --> 01:17:30.880
How has Africa fared being
outside of the globalization

01:17:30.880 --> 01:17:32.370
system?

01:17:32.370 --> 01:17:36.580
What converted
Hobbes' jungle, where

01:17:36.580 --> 01:17:39.700
life was short,
nasty, and brutish,

01:17:39.700 --> 01:17:42.970
into the present world?

01:17:42.970 --> 01:17:43.900
It was Newton.

01:17:43.900 --> 01:17:47.820
It was the age after Newton.

01:17:47.820 --> 01:17:55.340
And there is no way that
the consumers of Walmart,

01:17:55.340 --> 01:17:59.060
given their quantum
of altruism, are

01:17:59.060 --> 01:18:03.370
going to give up their
comfortable standard of living

01:18:03.370 --> 01:18:07.540
in order to have a
unilateral transfer, a gift,

01:18:07.540 --> 01:18:08.320
to those workers.

01:18:08.320 --> 01:18:12.190
And so you have to ask
yourself, in the next 10 years,

01:18:12.190 --> 01:18:15.860
will the emerging
market countries

01:18:15.860 --> 01:18:21.420
have an upward trend in
real wages for their people,

01:18:21.420 --> 01:18:27.090
earned by their people in
the exchange system or not?

01:18:27.090 --> 01:18:33.030
And a simple-minded
support of globalization

01:18:33.030 --> 01:18:35.220
under all conditions is wrong.

01:18:35.220 --> 01:18:38.820
But a simple-minded
rejection of it--

01:18:38.820 --> 01:18:41.370
now, I said I was going
to be nonpartisan.

01:18:41.370 --> 01:18:45.166
But I do have to say, I don't
agree with candidate Buchanan.

01:18:45.166 --> 01:18:51.120
[LAUGHTER]

01:18:51.120 --> 01:18:54.270
AUDIENCE: I have a question
for Professor Modigliani.

01:18:54.270 --> 01:18:56.340
You talk about changing
the pension system

01:18:56.340 --> 01:18:58.140
from the current
system to a system

01:18:58.140 --> 01:19:03.810
where you fund, basically,
your pensions through time,

01:19:03.810 --> 01:19:06.670
you start investing them now.

01:19:06.670 --> 01:19:09.580
One of the places where you
would invest those pensions

01:19:09.580 --> 01:19:11.545
would be the stock market.

01:19:11.545 --> 01:19:13.170
And earlier in your
remarks, you talked

01:19:13.170 --> 01:19:16.320
about sort of the irrationality
that's inherent in the stock

01:19:16.320 --> 01:19:17.036
market currently.

01:19:17.036 --> 01:19:17.910
MODIGLIANI: Yes, yes.

01:19:17.910 --> 01:19:19.951
AUDIENCE: So I'm wondering
if you could elaborate

01:19:19.951 --> 01:19:24.630
a little bit on and
how you would insulate,

01:19:24.630 --> 01:19:27.390
how you would insulate the
funds for those pensions

01:19:27.390 --> 01:19:30.296
from the stock market volatility
and how you would make sure

01:19:30.296 --> 01:19:31.920
that people actually
do get their money

01:19:31.920 --> 01:19:34.781
at the end of their lives.

01:19:34.781 --> 01:19:36.780
MODIGLIANI: Let me answer
briefly because that's

01:19:36.780 --> 01:19:37.500
the time we have.

01:19:37.500 --> 01:19:42.300
First of all, let's be
clear that in my system,

01:19:42.300 --> 01:19:45.120
all the money that's
contributed goes

01:19:45.120 --> 01:19:49.770
not into your own portfolio, it
goes into a common portfolio.

01:19:49.770 --> 01:19:55.300
The idea, to be
nonpartisan, of Bush

01:19:55.300 --> 01:19:58.010
that it should go into
your own portfolio

01:19:58.010 --> 01:20:01.560
is completely wrong because
it makes Social Security

01:20:01.560 --> 01:20:06.010
into a gambling casino,
OK, exactly what it does.

01:20:06.010 --> 01:20:09.810
[APPLAUSE]

01:20:09.810 --> 01:20:11.160
You are forced to save.

01:20:11.160 --> 01:20:12.750
You should know what comes out.

01:20:12.750 --> 01:20:15.660
And that requires
defined benefits.

01:20:15.660 --> 01:20:19.800
Now, how do I invest the
money that's accumulated?

01:20:19.800 --> 01:20:25.650
Essential in a portfolio
that mimics the economy

01:20:25.650 --> 01:20:29.980
composed of the same
way as the market.

01:20:29.980 --> 01:20:33.640
Now that doesn't prevent the
market from some movements.

01:20:33.640 --> 01:20:35.790
And right now, it's too high.

01:20:35.790 --> 01:20:38.200
The specific problem
that it is now too high

01:20:38.200 --> 01:20:40.630
I handle the following way.

01:20:40.630 --> 01:20:44.470
Before my reform is
accepted, there'll

01:20:44.470 --> 01:20:49.250
be plenty of time for
the bubble to deflate.

01:20:49.250 --> 01:20:52.273
Then, when it's
deflated, we will invest.

01:20:52.273 --> 01:20:54.205
[LAUGHTER]

01:20:58.560 --> 01:21:02.770
AUDIENCE: I'd like to come back
to the globalization topic.

01:21:02.770 --> 01:21:04.980
The reason, at least
from my point of view,

01:21:04.980 --> 01:21:08.940
is that many of the products
you can buy in Wal-Mart

01:21:08.940 --> 01:21:12.300
are so cheap is because
low wages are very

01:21:12.300 --> 01:21:15.920
low in developing countries,
but a reason for that

01:21:15.920 --> 01:21:19.170
is that, in large part, because
of repressive governments,

01:21:19.170 --> 01:21:24.810
such as in Indonesia or in
Latin America, for example.

01:21:24.810 --> 01:21:27.601
And the US and Europe,
to some extent,

01:21:27.601 --> 01:21:29.100
supports these
governments, actually

01:21:29.100 --> 01:21:30.224
nurtures these governments.

01:21:30.224 --> 01:21:32.790
Because usually corporations
get a higher yield out

01:21:32.790 --> 01:21:34.420
of a dictatorship.

01:21:34.420 --> 01:21:36.750
So in a sense, there is
some unfairness there.

01:21:36.750 --> 01:21:40.990
Because these repressive
governments actually,

01:21:40.990 --> 01:21:45.690
well, they attack unions and
they attack labor movements.

01:21:45.690 --> 01:21:49.740
And so this is actually a
very unfair form, reason

01:21:49.740 --> 01:21:52.220
why these wages are so low.

01:21:52.220 --> 01:21:54.690
Because the labor, power
of labor in these countries

01:21:54.690 --> 01:21:57.810
is very weak
because of violence.

01:21:57.810 --> 01:22:02.040
And I'd like to know, hear what
you have to say about that.

01:22:02.040 --> 01:22:07.620
SOLOW: Well, let me
take a shot at that.

01:22:07.620 --> 01:22:10.020
I want to separate two things.

01:22:10.020 --> 01:22:16.660
The fundamental reason why wages
are low in developing countries

01:22:16.660 --> 01:22:20.610
is because productivity
is low in those countries.

01:22:20.610 --> 01:22:23.550
That's far more-- if
you were to-- if you

01:22:23.550 --> 01:22:29.400
were to classify poor
countries or all the countries

01:22:29.400 --> 01:22:34.890
of the world in terms of
the degree of democracy

01:22:34.890 --> 01:22:38.190
that's available,
the correlation

01:22:38.190 --> 01:22:43.830
between that and earning
power is very small.

01:22:43.830 --> 01:22:46.940
The fundamental
reason for the poverty

01:22:46.940 --> 01:22:51.960
of underdeveloped,
undeveloped countries

01:22:51.960 --> 01:22:55.610
is their low productivity.

01:22:55.610 --> 01:22:59.390
Bad governance,
dictatorships, tyrannies

01:22:59.390 --> 01:23:03.120
of one kind or another
surely make things worse.

01:23:03.120 --> 01:23:05.420
But if we're going to be--

01:23:05.420 --> 01:23:08.270
deal rationally with
the world economy,

01:23:08.270 --> 01:23:13.610
we have to distinguish
between the globalization

01:23:13.610 --> 01:23:20.420
in the sense of open trade
where possible, migration

01:23:20.420 --> 01:23:24.560
possibilities, free
movement of capital

01:23:24.560 --> 01:23:27.980
from places where there's
a lot of it to places

01:23:27.980 --> 01:23:29.590
where there's a little of it.

01:23:29.590 --> 01:23:36.050
That has to be distinguished
from dictatorship, tyranny,

01:23:36.050 --> 01:23:39.150
lack of democracy.

01:23:39.150 --> 01:23:41.390
From my own point
of view, I think

01:23:41.390 --> 01:23:47.390
that over the past 50 years,
American foreign policy

01:23:47.390 --> 01:23:53.540
has been much too tolerant of
dictatorships and repressive

01:23:53.540 --> 01:23:56.310
governments in the third world.

01:23:56.310 --> 01:23:57.820
And I have certainly--

01:23:57.820 --> 01:24:03.830
and I imagine all of us here
have opposed that politically.

01:24:03.830 --> 01:24:09.380
But the step from
that to saying one

01:24:09.380 --> 01:24:16.970
ought to restrict trade with
poor countries is a bad step.

01:24:16.970 --> 01:24:17.930
It's an illogical step.

01:24:20.960 --> 01:24:23.540
Generally it's the
representatives

01:24:23.540 --> 01:24:32.580
of workers in poor countries who
want open trade, not oppose it.

01:24:32.580 --> 01:24:36.440
So I think we should try to
keep the political side of this

01:24:36.440 --> 01:24:41.570
from the economic side of
it separate intellectually.

01:24:41.570 --> 01:24:46.160
Because otherwise, it's not
possible to think rationally

01:24:46.160 --> 01:24:49.880
about those things.

01:24:49.880 --> 01:24:53.180
SAMUELSON: I would not
minimize the harmful effects

01:24:53.180 --> 01:24:57.436
upon future standards of living
of repressive government.

01:24:57.436 --> 01:25:02.730
It'd be very difficult
in a Nigeria,

01:25:02.730 --> 01:25:07.950
even though it had oil, in
the Congo, rich with metals,

01:25:07.950 --> 01:25:09.600
to develop at all.

01:25:09.600 --> 01:25:15.090
And when colonial powers
moved out, at the beginning,

01:25:15.090 --> 01:25:17.220
they were not
repressive governments.

01:25:17.220 --> 01:25:19.440
But without the
repressive governments,

01:25:19.440 --> 01:25:24.050
they didn't spring instantly
into high wage countries.

01:25:24.050 --> 01:25:29.160
If you just begin the process
and the international division

01:25:29.160 --> 01:25:33.960
of labor can help when there
isn't a repressive government

01:25:33.960 --> 01:25:38.730
and it won't help much where
there is such repressive

01:25:38.730 --> 01:25:42.720
government that nobody feels
secure in doing any saving,

01:25:42.720 --> 01:25:45.450
nobody gets the result
of their productivity,

01:25:45.450 --> 01:25:47.100
there is no way of
sending students

01:25:47.100 --> 01:25:51.570
to get advanced
training, so there always

01:25:51.570 --> 01:25:57.690
are an eclectic number of
explanations to each problem.

01:25:57.690 --> 01:26:00.660
That's why economics is boring.

01:26:00.660 --> 01:26:05.240
But to me, that's
why it's interesting.

01:26:05.240 --> 01:26:08.230
AUDIENCE: Professor Solow,
this is mostly for you.

01:26:08.230 --> 01:26:11.560
With the new productivity
numbers between 2% and 3%

01:26:11.560 --> 01:26:14.850
annually, what do you see as
the correct Fed's position

01:26:14.850 --> 01:26:17.550
on interest rates, which
is currently at 6.50%?

01:26:17.550 --> 01:26:20.610
But historically speaking,
that's over-inflated.

01:26:20.610 --> 01:26:23.190
What is your opinion?

01:26:23.190 --> 01:26:25.996
SOLOW: About interest
rates here in the US or--

01:26:25.996 --> 01:26:27.870
AUDIENCE: Yes, interest
rates here in the US.

01:26:27.870 --> 01:26:35.640
SOLOW: Well, the Federal
Reserve, the central bank

01:26:35.640 --> 01:26:40.920
in the US has, I think, in
formulating and executing

01:26:40.920 --> 01:26:46.710
monetary policy has been
well-aware and reflective

01:26:46.710 --> 01:26:51.300
about the growth
of productivity.

01:26:51.300 --> 01:26:55.980
They may occasionally have
been overoptimistic, in fact.

01:26:55.980 --> 01:27:00.406
But on the whole,
I think that as--

01:27:04.320 --> 01:27:07.644
are you ready for a
complicated answer here?

01:27:07.644 --> 01:27:08.560
AUDIENCE: It's my job.

01:27:08.560 --> 01:27:10.950
I can take a complicated answer.

01:27:10.950 --> 01:27:14.730
SOLOW: There is a
real danger that--

01:27:14.730 --> 01:27:17.160
a real possibility,
I think, that we

01:27:17.160 --> 01:27:23.280
are managing this period of
rapid expansion with little

01:27:23.280 --> 01:27:30.600
or no inflation as a result
of an unexpected acceleration

01:27:30.600 --> 01:27:36.930
of productivity, which has not
been fully reflected in wages

01:27:36.930 --> 01:27:41.400
and therefore in--

01:27:41.400 --> 01:27:44.190
and therefore prices
are able to move along

01:27:44.190 --> 01:27:46.110
at a relatively low level.

01:27:46.110 --> 01:27:52.500
I think the Fed has performed
extraordinarily well in seeing

01:27:52.500 --> 01:27:55.440
that this lucky
period-- if it's luck--

01:27:55.440 --> 01:27:58.980
is translated into high
employment and increasing

01:27:58.980 --> 01:28:01.080
production.

01:28:01.080 --> 01:28:11.000
They are nervous, as we're
all nervous, that the timing,

01:28:11.000 --> 01:28:16.290
when this particular
lucky period ends,

01:28:16.290 --> 01:28:21.210
the timing is going to be
very difficult to get right.

01:28:21.210 --> 01:28:24.620
So hold your breath.

01:28:24.620 --> 01:28:26.580
AUDIENCE: OK.

01:28:26.580 --> 01:28:30.210
MODIGLIANI: Can I just
add a word to this?

01:28:30.210 --> 01:28:33.240
I think the Federal
Reserve on the whole

01:28:33.240 --> 01:28:37.200
has done a really excellent job.

01:28:37.200 --> 01:28:39.910
They may have been
lucky to some extent,

01:28:39.910 --> 01:28:41.460
but they have shown
the right mixture

01:28:41.460 --> 01:28:44.080
of courage and restraint.

01:28:44.080 --> 01:28:46.600
I think they're
really quite good.

01:28:46.600 --> 01:28:49.310
And I think-- when I
said before that Europe

01:28:49.310 --> 01:28:52.850
has a lot of unemployment and
they don't understand Keynes,

01:28:52.850 --> 01:28:57.570
one of the manifestation of this
is that the European Central

01:28:57.570 --> 01:29:04.430
Bank has only one
instruction, avoid inflation,

01:29:04.430 --> 01:29:07.540
whereas the American
Constitution says,

01:29:07.540 --> 01:29:10.880
see to it that output
is large as possible

01:29:10.880 --> 01:29:12.970
subject to the
condition that there

01:29:12.970 --> 01:29:14.845
should be minimum inflation.

01:29:14.845 --> 01:29:16.220
And that makes a
good difference.

01:29:16.220 --> 01:29:20.790
And I think we see that
policy being pursued here.

01:29:20.790 --> 01:29:23.180
And you see the policy
of saying no inflation

01:29:23.180 --> 01:29:25.910
leads to very tight
and high unemployment.

01:29:25.910 --> 01:29:27.140
Because then you're sure.

01:29:27.140 --> 01:29:28.940
If you've got 20%
unemployed, then you

01:29:28.940 --> 01:29:31.570
don't have to worry
about inflation.

01:29:31.570 --> 01:29:33.870
SOLOW: I'm afraid we're
going to have to--

01:29:33.870 --> 01:29:36.280
to stop now.

01:29:36.280 --> 01:29:40.840
And although my understanding
is there's going to be some food

01:29:40.840 --> 01:29:43.270
and drink outside
after we're done.

01:29:43.270 --> 01:29:46.030
And I at least, and I
imagine my colleagues,

01:29:46.030 --> 01:29:49.120
are happy to stand around
and chat some more.

01:29:49.120 --> 01:29:56.830
But the introductions are going
to go in flow up hill here.

01:29:56.830 --> 01:30:02.380
I would like to introduce
to you Marty Zimmerman, who

01:30:02.380 --> 01:30:04.840
is the Vice President
for Government

01:30:04.840 --> 01:30:07.750
Relations of the
Ford Motor Company,

01:30:07.750 --> 01:30:12.760
is a PhD in economics
from this institution,

01:30:12.760 --> 01:30:19.540
and is, in large part,
responsible for the program

01:30:19.540 --> 01:30:23.170
of which this
evening is the start.

01:30:23.170 --> 01:30:24.090
Marty.

01:30:24.090 --> 01:30:24.590
Thank you.

01:30:24.590 --> 01:30:27.950
[APPLAUSE]

01:30:31.800 --> 01:30:33.000
ZIMMERMAN: Thank you, Bob.

01:30:33.000 --> 01:30:34.583
I was hoping you
wouldn't introduce me

01:30:34.583 --> 01:30:37.890
and then I could use my line a
la Admiral Stockdale, who am I

01:30:37.890 --> 01:30:40.430
and what am I doing here?

01:30:40.430 --> 01:30:42.180
But having introduced
me, I just want

01:30:42.180 --> 01:30:44.370
to express my
thanks to the panel,

01:30:44.370 --> 01:30:47.880
to professors Samuelson, Solow,
and Modigliani for a really

01:30:47.880 --> 01:30:49.020
interesting evening.

01:30:49.020 --> 01:30:51.810
I'm thrilled, personally,
to be part of this.

01:30:51.810 --> 01:30:55.200
As Professor Solow has said, I
was a student mid-century here,

01:30:55.200 --> 01:30:57.780
mid-century in terms
of the between the '50

01:30:57.780 --> 01:31:00.470
and the 2000 period.

01:31:00.470 --> 01:31:02.460
And I derived
tremendous benefits

01:31:02.460 --> 01:31:04.290
from studying from
these gentlemen.

01:31:04.290 --> 01:31:08.490
I think we all got a taste
tonight of what an enriching

01:31:08.490 --> 01:31:09.390
experience that was.

01:31:09.390 --> 01:31:11.400
And I don't mean solely
in Franco's terms

01:31:11.400 --> 01:31:15.320
that we all found ourselves
wealthier in the year 2000.

01:31:15.320 --> 01:31:18.704
Although as the developer of
the life-cycle hypothesis,

01:31:18.704 --> 01:31:20.370
do you feel that you
consume too little?

01:31:24.159 --> 01:31:25.200
MODIGLIANI: That's right.

01:31:25.200 --> 01:31:26.825
ZIMMERMAN: Anyway,
Ford Motor Company--

01:31:29.114 --> 01:31:31.155
MODIGLIANI: I'm going to
go consume more outside.

01:31:31.155 --> 01:31:34.505
[LAUGHTER]

01:31:34.505 --> 01:31:35.880
ZIMMERMAN: But
Ford Motor Company

01:31:35.880 --> 01:31:38.700
is really delighted
to be able to sponsor

01:31:38.700 --> 01:31:41.040
this series of Nobel laureates.

01:31:41.040 --> 01:31:43.500
I think this was a
terrific kick-off.

01:31:43.500 --> 01:31:47.130
I hope, but doubt, that
we'll be able to sustain

01:31:47.130 --> 01:31:50.910
this level of quality as
we go through this series.

01:31:50.910 --> 01:31:53.460
This is one part, and
really a small part,

01:31:53.460 --> 01:31:57.210
of a very deep and evolving
relationship between Ford Motor

01:31:57.210 --> 01:31:57.810
Company--

01:31:57.810 --> 01:31:59.850
by the way when I
come to Cambridge,

01:31:59.850 --> 01:32:02.980
it always helps recognition if
I mention that Ford owns Volvo.

01:32:02.980 --> 01:32:09.600
So that's-- but it is really
only one part of a, as I said,

01:32:09.600 --> 01:32:13.140
a deep and evolving
relationship involving research

01:32:13.140 --> 01:32:16.440
and teaching, which
I am excited about.

01:32:16.440 --> 01:32:19.050
We are-- I know at Ford
we're very excited about it.

01:32:19.050 --> 01:32:22.029
And I believe our
counterparts here at MIT

01:32:22.029 --> 01:32:24.070
are excited because we've
done some great things.

01:32:24.070 --> 01:32:26.760
And we look forward to
doing even greater things

01:32:26.760 --> 01:32:27.690
in the future.

01:32:27.690 --> 01:32:29.370
My second role
tonight is to tell you

01:32:29.370 --> 01:32:31.590
that there is a
reception outside.

01:32:31.590 --> 01:32:33.560
Since we've had such
a tremendous turnout--

01:32:33.560 --> 01:32:36.060
and, in fact, I was told
there were overflow crowds

01:32:36.060 --> 01:32:38.010
in several parts of campus--

01:32:38.010 --> 01:32:39.840
we've moved the
reception outside.

01:32:39.840 --> 01:32:41.790
I trust the weather
is cooperating.

01:32:41.790 --> 01:32:43.290
So, again, thank
you all for coming.

01:32:43.290 --> 01:32:44.873
And, again, thank
you to the panelists

01:32:44.873 --> 01:32:46.050
for a truly great evening.

01:32:46.050 --> 01:32:50.600
[APPLAUSE]

